{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <iostream>\n\nvoid bubbleSort(std::vector<int>& arr) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < arr.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = 0; j < arr.size() - i - 1; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (arr[j] > arr[j + 1]) {\n\t\t\t\tint temp = arr[j];\n\t\t\t\tarr[j] = arr[j + 1];\n\t\t\t\tarr[j + 1] = temp;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid optimizedSort(std::vector<int>& arr) {\n\tstd::sort(arr.begin(), arr.end());\n}\n---\nThe original bubble sort has a time complexity of O(n^2), which is inefficient for large datasets. Replacing it with std::sort utilizes Introsort, which provides O(n log n) performance. This significantly reduces CPU cycles and leverages optimized partitioning logic."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortColors(std::vector<int>& nums) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < nums.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < nums.size(); j++) {\n\t\t\tif (nums[i] > nums[j]) {\n\t\t\t\tstd::swap(nums[i], nums[j]);\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortColors(std::vector<int>& nums) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = static_cast<int>(nums.size()) - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (nums[mid] == 0) {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(nums[low++], nums[mid++]);\n\t\t} else if (nums[mid] == 1) {\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(nums[mid], nums[high--]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nThe original nested loop approach results in O(n^2) time complexity. Since the input only contains three distinct values (0, 1, 2), the Dutch National Flag algorithm provides a single-pass O(n) solution. This eliminates redundant comparisons and minimizes writes by using three pointers."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <string>\n\nstruct Product {\n\tint id;\n\tdouble price;\n};\n\nvoid sortByPrice(std::vector<Product>& prods) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < prods.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = 0; j < prods.size() - 1; ++j) {\n\t\t\tif (prods[j].price > prods[j+1].price) {\n\t\t\t\tstd::swap(prods[j], prods[j+1]);\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstruct Product {\n\tint id;\n\tdouble price;\n};\n\nvoid sortByPrice(std::vector<Product>& prods) {\n\tstd::sort(prods.begin(), prods.end(), [](const Product& a, const Product& b) {\n\t\treturn a.price < b.price;\n\t});\n}\n---\nManual bubble sort of structs is O(n^2) and performs heavy object copies during swaps. Using std::sort with a lambda comparator reduces complexity to O(n log n). The standard library implementation is also better at minimizing the overhead of moving complex data types."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid selectionSort(std::vector<int>& data) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < data.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tsize_t min_idx = i;\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < data.size(); ++j) {\n\t\t\tif (data[j] < data[min_idx]) min_idx = j;\n\t\t}\n\t\tstd::swap(data[i], data[min_idx]);\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid optimizedSort(std::vector<int>& data) {\n\tstd::sort(data.begin(), data.end());\n}\n---\nSelection sort always runs in O(n^2) time regardless of the initial order of the elements. std::sort provides O(n log n) average and worst-case performance, which is exponentially faster for large lists. It also benefits from cache-friendly partitioning algorithms."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstd::vector<int> getTopK(std::vector<int> nums, int k) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < nums.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = 0; j < nums.size() - 1; ++j) {\n\t\t\tif (nums[j] < nums[j + 1]) std::swap(nums[j], nums[j + 1]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tnums.resize(k);\n\treturn nums;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstd::vector<int> getTopK(std::vector<int> nums, int k) {\n\tif (k >= nums.size()) {\n\t\tstd::sort(nums.begin(), nums.end(), std::greater<int>());\n\t\treturn nums;\n\t}\n\tstd::nth_element(nums.begin(), nums.begin() + k, nums.end(), std::greater<int>());\n\tnums.resize(k);\n\tstd::sort(nums.begin(), nums.end(), std::greater<int>());\n\treturn nums;\n}\n---\nSorting the entire array O(n log n) or O(n^2) to find only K elements is wasteful. std::nth_element finds the top K elements in O(n) average time. This avoids sorting the remaining N-K elements, leading to significant performance gains in selection tasks."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortBinary(std::vector<int>& arr) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < arr.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < arr.size(); j++) {\n\t\t\tif (arr[i] > arr[j]) std::swap(arr[i], arr[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortBinary(std::vector<int>& arr) {\n\tint low = 0, high = static_cast<int>(arr.size()) - 1;\n\twhile (low < high) {\n\t\tif (arr[low] == 0) low++;\n\t\telse if (arr[high] == 1) high--;\n\t\telse std::swap(arr[low], arr[high]);\n\t}\n}\n---\nSorting binary values with O(n^2) comparison-based sorts is inefficient. A two-pointer approach partitions the array in O(n) time with a single pass. This reduces the number of comparisons and swaps significantly compared to generic sorting algorithms."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortSmallRange(std::vector<int>& nums) {\n\tstd::sort(nums.begin(), nums.end());\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortSmallRange(std::vector<int>& nums) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = static_cast<int>(nums.size()) - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (nums[mid] == 0) {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(nums[low++], nums[mid++]);\n\t\t} else if (nums[mid] == 1) {\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(nums[mid], nums[high--]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nWhile std::sort is O(n log n), the Dutch National Flag algorithm handles ranges with only 3 unique values in O(n) time. It uses three pointers to partition elements in place without extra memory. This is the optimal strategy for sorting small-range integer sets like {0, 1, 2}."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <iostream>\n\nvoid insertionSort(std::vector<int>& vec) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 1; i < vec.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tint key = vec[i];\n\t\tint j = i - 1;\n\t\twhile (j >= 0 && vec[j] > key) {\n\t\t\tvec[j + 1] = vec[j];\n\t\t\tj--;\n\t\t}\n\t\tvec[j + 1] = key;\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid optimizedSort(std::vector<int>& vec) {\n\tstd::sort(vec.begin(), vec.end());\n}\n---\nInsertion sort is O(n^2) in the average case and becomes very slow as N grows. std::sort provides O(n log n) efficiency by combining quicksort, heapsort, and insertion sort (Introsort). This hybrid approach ensures consistent performance regardless of data distribution."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortZeroOneTwo(int arr[], int n) {\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < n - i - 1; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (arr[j] > arr[j+1]) {\n\t\t\t\tint t = arr[j];\n\t\t\t\tarr[j] = arr[j+1];\n\t\t\t\tarr[j+1] = t;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortZeroOneTwo(int arr[], int n) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = n - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (arr[mid] == 0) {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(arr[low++], arr[mid++]);\n\t\t} else if (arr[mid] == 1) {\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(arr[mid], arr[high--]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nThe O(n^2) bubble sort is redundant for data containing only three specific values. The Dutch National Flag algorithm partitions the array into three sections in O(n) time. This minimizes swaps and requires no auxiliary space, improving performance from quadratic to linear time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <string>\n\nstruct Item {\n\tstd::string name;\n\tint priority;\n};\n\nvoid sortItems(std::vector<Item>& items) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < items.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < items.size(); ++j) {\n\t\t\tif (items[i].priority < items[j].priority) {\n\t\t\t\tstd::swap(items[i], items[j]);\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n#include <string>\n\nstruct Item {\n\tstd::string name;\n\tint priority;\n};\n\nvoid sortItems(std::vector<Item>& items) {\n\tstd::sort(items.begin(), items.end(), [](const Item& a, const Item& b) {\n\t\treturn a.priority > b.priority;\n\t});\n}\n---\nThe original O(n^2) selection-style sort is inefficient for large vectors of structs. std::sort is O(n log n) and provides better move semantics, which is important when swapping structs containing std::string. This minimizes memory allocation overhead and total execution time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid partialSort(std::vector<int>& vec, int k) {\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < k; ++i) {\n\t\tsize_t min_idx = i;\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < vec.size(); ++j) {\n\t\t\tif (vec[j] < vec[min_idx]) min_idx = j;\n\t\t}\n\t\tstd::swap(vec[i], vec[min_idx]);\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid partialSort(std::vector<int>& vec, int k) {\n\tif (k < vec.size()) {\n\t\tstd::partial_sort(vec.begin(), vec.begin() + k, vec.end());\n\t}\n}\n---\nManual selection sort to find top elements is O(n * k). std::partial_sort uses a heap-based approach to achieve O(n log k) complexity. This is significantly more efficient for large N, reducing the total number of comparisons and swaps needed to order the first K elements."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortFlags(std::vector<int>& flags) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < flags.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = 0; j < flags.size() - 1; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (flags[j] > flags[j+1]) std::swap(flags[j], flags[j+1]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortFlags(std::vector<int>& flags) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = static_cast<int>(flags.size()) - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (flags[mid] == 0) {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(flags[low++], flags[mid++]);\n\t\t} else if (flags[mid] == 1) {\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(flags[mid], flags[high--]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nUsing O(n^2) bubble sort for values restricted to {0, 1, 2} is suboptimal. The Dutch National Flag algorithm achieves O(n) time complexity by partitioning the data in one pass. It reduces comparison overhead and operates in-place without auxiliary storage."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortNearlySorted(std::vector<int>& nums) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 1; i < nums.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tint val = nums[i];\n\t\tint j = i - 1;\n\t\twhile (j >= 0 && nums[j] > val) {\n\t\t\tnums[j + 1] = nums[j];\n\t\t\tj--;\n\t\t}\n\t\tnums[j + 1] = val;\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortNearlySorted(std::vector<int>& nums) {\n\tstd::sort(nums.begin(), nums.end());\n}\n---\nWhile insertion sort is O(n) for strictly sorted data, it quickly degrades to O(n^2) for general cases. std::sort (Introsort) is O(n log n) in the worst case and is highly optimized for modern hardware. It manages cache locality better than a manual insertion loop for larger datasets."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nstruct Student {\n\tint grade;\n\tint id;\n};\n\nvoid sortByGrade(std::vector<Student>& students) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < students.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = 0; j < students.size() - 1; ++j) {\n\t\t\tif (students[j].grade > students[j+1].grade) {\n\t\t\t\tstd::swap(students[j], students[j+1]);\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstruct Student {\n\tint grade;\n\tint id;\n};\n\nvoid sortByGrade(std::vector<Student>& students) {\n\tstd::stable_sort(students.begin(), students.end(), [](const Student& a, const Student& b) {\n\t\treturn a.grade < b.grade;\n\t});\n}\n---\nBubble sort is O(n^2) and manual swaps of structs can be slow. std::stable_sort provides O(n log n) complexity and preserves the relative order of students with the same grade. Using standard library algorithms also enables compiler optimizations like loop unrolling and SIMD."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortThreeVals(std::vector<int>& v) {\n\tstd::sort(v.begin(), v.end());\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortThreeVals(std::vector<int>& v) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = static_cast<int>(v.size()) - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (v[mid] == 0) {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(v[low++], v[mid++]);\n\t\t} else if (v[mid] == 1) {\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(v[mid], v[high--]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nIf the dataset only contains 0, 1, and 2, std::sort is overkill at O(n log n). The Dutch National Flag algorithm provides a single-pass O(n) solution using three pointers. This avoids the log factors of comparison-based sorting and minimizes memory bandwidth usage."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid selectionSortTop(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < k; ++i) {\n\t\tsize_t min_idx = i;\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < nums.size(); ++j) {\n\t\t\tif (nums[j] < nums[min_idx]) min_idx = j;\n\t\t}\n\t\tstd::swap(nums[i], nums[min_idx]);\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid selectionSortTop(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tif (k < nums.size()) {\n\t\tstd::partial_sort(nums.begin(), nums.begin() + k, nums.end());\n\t}\n}\n---\nManual selection sort for the first K elements is O(n * k), which becomes inefficient as K increases. std::partial_sort uses a max-heap to achieve O(n log k) performance, which scales much better for large datasets. It minimizes the number of comparisons and writes by only ordering the necessary portion."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortColorsSinglePass(std::vector<int>& arr) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < arr.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < arr.size(); j++) {\n\t\t\tif (arr[i] > arr[j]) std::swap(arr[i], arr[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortColorsSinglePass(std::vector<int>& arr) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = static_cast<int>(arr.size()) - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (arr[mid] == 0) {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(arr[low++], arr[mid++]);\n\t\t} else if (arr[mid] == 1) {\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(arr[mid], arr[high--]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nNested loops for three-value sorting result in O(n^2) complexity. The Dutch National Flag algorithm is O(n) and completes the sort in a single pass over the data. This significantly reduces the constant factors associated with branching and memory access patterns."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <iostream>\n\nvoid slowSort(std::vector<int>& v) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < v.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = 0; j < v.size() - 1; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (v[j] > v[j+1]) std::swap(v[j], v[j+1]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid fastSort(std::vector<int>& v) {\n\tstd::sort(v.begin(), v.end());\n}\n---\nBubble sort is O(n^2) and creates massive overhead for large arrays. std::sort uses Introsort to guarantee O(n log n) time complexity. Standard library algorithms are also optimized for memory access patterns, which leads to better cache utilization."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortRGB(std::vector<int>& rgb) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < rgb.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < rgb.size(); j++) {\n\t\t\tif (rgb[i] > rgb[j]) std::swap(rgb[i], rgb[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortRGB(std::vector<int>& rgb) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = static_cast<int>(rgb.size()) - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (rgb[mid] == 0) {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(rgb[low++], rgb[mid++]);\n\t\t} else if (rgb[mid] == 1) {\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(rgb[mid], rgb[high--]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nThe original O(n^2) implementation performs unnecessary comparisons. The Dutch National Flag algorithm partitions the 0s, 1s, and 2s in O(n) time. This optimization is crucial when handling discrete sets of values as it avoids the overhead of generic sorting algorithms."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstruct Data {\n\tint val;\n\tchar label;\n};\n\nvoid sortData(std::vector<Data>& arr) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < arr.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < arr.size(); j++) {\n\t\t\tif (arr[i].val > arr[j].val) std::swap(arr[i], arr[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstruct Data {\n\tint val;\n\tchar label;\n};\n\nvoid sortData(std::vector<Data>& arr) {\n\tstd::sort(arr.begin(), arr.end(), [](const Data& a, const Data& b) {\n\t\treturn a.val < b.val;\n\t});\n}\n---\nO(n^2) comparison sort is inefficient for struct arrays. std::sort provides O(n log n) efficiency and uses optimized block-moving logic for swapping structs. This results in faster execution and fewer memory accesses during the sorting process."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid selectionSortSimple(std::vector<int>& v) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < v.size(); i++) {\n\t\tsize_t m = i;\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < v.size(); j++) {\n\t\t\tif (v[j] < v[m]) m = j;\n\t\t}\n\t\tstd::swap(v[i], v[m]);\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid optimizedSort(std::vector<int>& v) {\n\tstd::sort(v.begin(), v.end());\n}\n---\nSelection sort is always O(n^2) and is not cache-efficient. std::sort is O(n log n) and uses highly tuned partitioning logic to minimize memory stall cycles. This optimization is vital for scaling performance as the size of the vector grows."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sort012(std::vector<int>& nums) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < nums.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = 0; j < nums.size() - 1; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (nums[j] > nums[j+1]) std::swap(nums[j], nums[j+1]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sort012(std::vector<int>& nums) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = static_cast<int>(nums.size()) - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (nums[mid] == 0) {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(nums[low++], nums[mid++]);\n\t\t} else if (nums[mid] == 1) {\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(nums[mid], nums[high--]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nThe quadratic O(n^2) bubble sort is extremely slow for discrete value sets. The Dutch National Flag algorithm partitions the array in O(n) time with a single pass. This improves performance by avoiding the redundant comparison cycles of generic sorting algorithms."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <string>\n\nstruct Record {\n\tint key;\n\tstd::string info;\n};\n\nvoid sortRecords(std::vector<Record>& r) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < r.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < r.size(); j++) {\n\t\t\tif (r[i].key > r[j].key) std::swap(r[i], r[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n#include <string>\n\nstruct Record {\n\tint key;\n\tstd::string info;\n};\n\nvoid sortRecords(std::vector<Record>& r) {\n\tstd::sort(r.begin(), r.end(), [](const Record& a, const Record& b) {\n\t\treturn a.key < b.key;\n\t});\n}\n---\nManual O(n^2) sorting on structs containing std::string is expensive due to frequent allocations during swaps. std::sort is O(n log n) and minimizes swap counts using advanced pivot selection. This drastically improves throughput for complex object collections."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortArray(int* arr, int n) {\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tfor (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (arr[i] > arr[j]) {\n\t\t\t\tint t = arr[i];\n\t\t\t\tarr[i] = arr[j];\n\t\t\t\tarr[j] = t;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortArray(int* arr, int n) {\n\tstd::sort(arr, arr + n);\n}\n---\nThe original selection sort algorithm is O(n^2), which is unsuitable for large pointers. std::sort leverages Introsort to achieve O(n log n) time complexity. Using the standard library implementation also allows the compiler to better optimize the sorting loop for the specific hardware."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortColorsNested(std::vector<int>& v) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < v.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < v.size(); j++) {\n\t\t\tif (v[i] > v[j]) std::swap(v[i], v[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortColorsNested(std::vector<int>& v) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = static_cast<int>(v.size()) - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (v[mid] == 0) {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(v[low++], v[mid++]);\n\t\t} else if (v[mid] == 1) {\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(v[mid], v[high--]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nThe nested loop approach for sorting three values results in O(n^2) time. The Dutch National Flag algorithm provides an O(n) solution using three pointers for a single-pass partition. This significantly reduces the time complexity and number of memory operations."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortNearly(std::vector<int>& data) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 1; i < data.size(); i++) {\n\t\tint k = data[i];\n\t\tint j = i - 1;\n\t\twhile (j >= 0 && data[j] > k) {\n\t\t\tdata[j+1] = data[j];\n\t\t\tj--;\n\t\t}\n\t\tdata[j+1] = k;\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortNearly(std::vector<int>& data) {\n\tstd::sort(data.begin(), data.end());\n}\n---\nInsertion sort is efficient only for very small or nearly sorted arrays; otherwise, it is O(n^2). std::sort provides O(n log n) complexity using a hybrid approach that is much more robust for general data distributions. This ensures better scalability and faster sorting times."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <string>\n\nstruct Task {\n\tint prio;\n\tstd::string desc;\n};\n\nvoid sortTasks(std::vector<Task>& t) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < t.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = 0; j < t.size() - 1; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (t[j].prio < t[j+1].prio) std::swap(t[j], t[j+1]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n#include <string>\n\nstruct Task {\n\tint prio;\n\tstd::string desc;\n};\n\nvoid sortTasks(std::vector<Task>& t) {\n\tstd::sort(t.begin(), t.end(), [](const Task& a, const Task& b) {\n\t\treturn a.prio > b.prio;\n\t});\n}\n---\nManual bubble sort is O(n^2) and performs excessive swaps, which are costly when structs contain strings. std::sort is O(n log n) and is optimized for the number of moves performed. This results in significantly faster execution when sorting prioritized objects."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortDNA(std::vector<int>& dna) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < dna.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < dna.size(); j++) {\n\t\t\tif (dna[i] > dna[j]) std::swap(dna[i], dna[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortDNA(std::vector<int>& dna) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = static_cast<int>(dna.size()) - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (dna[mid] == 0) {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(dna[low++], dna[mid++]);\n\t\t} else if (dna[mid] == 1) {\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(dna[mid], dna[high--]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nSorting values that only fall into three categories using O(n^2) logic is inefficient. The Dutch National Flag algorithm partitions the data in O(n) time. This single-pass optimization drastically improves the speed of processing arrays with low cardinality."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid selectionSortLarge(std::vector<int>& vec) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < vec.size(); i++) {\n\t\tsize_t idx = i;\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < vec.size(); j++) {\n\t\t\tif (vec[j] < vec[idx]) idx = j;\n\t\t}\n\t\tstd::swap(vec[i], vec[idx]);\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid selectionSortLarge(std::vector<int>& vec) {\n\tstd::sort(vec.begin(), vec.end());\n}\n---\nSelection sort is O(n^2) and does not take advantage of modern CPU cache architectures. std::sort is O(n log n) and uses cache-oblivious algorithms to minimize memory latency. This leads to a massive reduction in sorting time for larger vectors."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortTriValues(std::vector<int>& nums) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < nums.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = 0; j < nums.size() - 1; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (nums[j] > nums[j+1]) std::swap(nums[j], nums[j+1]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortTriValues(std::vector<int>& nums) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = static_cast<int>(nums.size()) - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (nums[mid] == 0) {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(nums[low++], nums[mid++]);\n\t\t} else if (nums[mid] == 1) {\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(nums[mid], nums[high--]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nThe O(n^2) bubble sort approach is suboptimal for small-range integers. The Dutch National Flag algorithm provides an O(n) partitioning logic that sorts the array in one pass. This optimizes both the CPU cycles and the memory bandwidth utilized."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <string>\n\nstruct Order {\n\tint timestamp;\n\tdouble amount;\n};\n\nvoid sortOrders(std::vector<Order>& orders) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < orders.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < orders.size(); j++) {\n\t\t\tif (orders[i].timestamp > orders[j].timestamp) {\n\t\t\t\tstd::swap(orders[i], orders[j]);\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstruct Order {\n\tint timestamp;\n\tdouble amount;\n};\n\nvoid sortOrders(std::vector<Order>& orders) {\n\tstd::sort(orders.begin(), orders.end(), [](const Order& a, const Order& b) {\n\t\treturn a.timestamp < b.timestamp;\n\t});\n}\n---\nManual O(n^2) sorting for structs is inefficient and slow. std::sort provides O(n log n) complexity and uses optimized swap logic that handles struct layouts effectively. This optimization is critical for processing large transaction or log datasets quickly."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid bubbleSortPointers(std::vector<int*>& ptrs) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < ptrs.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = 0; j < ptrs.size() - 1; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (*ptrs[j] > *ptrs[j+1]) std::swap(ptrs[j], ptrs[j+1]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortPointers(std::vector<int*>& ptrs) {\n\tstd::sort(ptrs.begin(), ptrs.end(), [](int* a, int* b) {\n\t\treturn *a < *b;\n\t});\n}\n---\nBubble sort of pointers is O(n^2) and incurs high latency due to pointer dereferencing in a slow loop. std::sort is O(n log n) and provides better comparison logic for pointer-based sorting. This significantly reduces the time spent in comparison branches and pointer chasing."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortThreeStates(std::vector<int>& v) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < v.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < v.size(); j++) {\n\t\t\tif (v[i] > v[j]) std::swap(v[i], v[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortThreeStates(std::vector<int>& v) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = static_cast<int>(v.size()) - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (v[mid] == 0) {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(v[low++], v[mid++]);\n\t\t} else if (v[mid] == 1) {\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(v[mid], v[high--]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nThe original O(n^2) selection-style sort is unnecessary for data with only three unique values. The Dutch National Flag algorithm sorts the array in O(n) time using single-pass partitioning. This optimization minimizes memory access and eliminates redundant comparisons."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid insertionSortVector(std::vector<double>& v) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 1; i < v.size(); i++) {\n\t\tdouble key = v[i];\n\t\tint j = i - 1;\n\t\twhile (j >= 0 && v[j] > key) {\n\t\t\tv[j + 1] = v[j];\n\t\t\tj--;\n\t\t}\n\t\tv[j + 1] = key;\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortVector(std::vector<double>& v) {\n\tstd::sort(v.begin(), v.end());\n}\n---\nInsertion sort is O(n^2) in the average case and becomes very slow as the vector size increases. std::sort is O(n log n) and is optimized for modern hardware, providing much faster sorting for double-precision floats. This ensures that the algorithm remains performant for large numeric arrays."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <string>\n\nstruct Entry {\n\tint key;\n\tchar data[64];\n};\n\nvoid sortEntries(std::vector<Entry>& entries) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < entries.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = 0; j < entries.size() - 1; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (entries[j].key > entries[j+1].key) std::swap(entries[j], entries[j+1]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstruct Entry {\n\tint key;\n\tchar data[64];\n};\n\nvoid sortEntries(std::vector<Entry>& entries) {\n\tstd::sort(entries.begin(), entries.end(), [](const Entry& a, const Entry& b) {\n\t\treturn a.key < b.key;\n\t});\n}\n---\nBubble sort is O(n^2) and swaps large structs repeatedly, which is highly inefficient. std::sort is O(n log n) and uses advanced pivot selection to minimize the number of object moves. This optimization significantly reduces both CPU instructions and cache pressure during sorting."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortSmallSet(std::vector<int>& nums) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < nums.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < nums.size(); j++) {\n\t\t\tif (nums[i] > nums[j]) std::swap(nums[i], nums[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortSmallSet(std::vector<int>& nums) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = static_cast<int>(nums.size()) - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (nums[mid] == 0) {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(nums[low++], nums[mid++]);\n\t\t} else if (nums[mid] == 1) {\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(nums[mid], nums[high--]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nO(n^2) sorting for integers that are limited to {0, 1, 2} is very slow. The Dutch National Flag algorithm partitions the values in O(n) time with a single pass. This linear-time solution is optimal for sorting arrays with a limited range of integer values."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid selectionSortInt(std::vector<int>& v) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < v.size(); i++) {\n\t\tsize_t min_idx = i;\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < v.size(); j++) {\n\t\t\tif (v[j] < v[min_idx]) min_idx = j;\n\t\t}\n\t\tstd::swap(v[i], v[min_idx]);\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid optimizedSort(std::vector<int>& v) {\n\tstd::sort(v.begin(), v.end());\n}\n---\nSelection sort is O(n^2) and performs poorly on modern CPUs due to limited instruction-level parallelism. std::sort provides O(n log n) efficiency and uses highly optimized partitioning logic to improve branch prediction and cache performance. This is essential for large vector sorting."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortBits(std::vector<int>& arr) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < arr.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = 0; j < arr.size() - 1; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (arr[j] > arr[j+1]) std::swap(arr[j], arr[j+1]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortBits(std::vector<int>& arr) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = static_cast<int>(arr.size()) - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (arr[mid] == 0) {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(arr[low++], arr[mid++]);\n\t\t} else if (arr[mid] == 1) {\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(arr[mid], arr[high--]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nThe O(n^2) bubble sort is extremely inefficient for values restricted to 0, 1, and 2. The Dutch National Flag algorithm provides an O(n) solution that requires only one pass and constant space. This optimization improves performance from quadratic to linear time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <string>\n\nstruct Event {\n\tint time;\n\tint type;\n};\n\nvoid sortEvents(std::vector<Event>& events) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < events.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < events.size(); j++) {\n\t\t\tif (events[i].time > events[j].time) std::swap(events[i], events[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstruct Event {\n\tint time;\n\tint type;\n};\n\nvoid sortEvents(std::vector<Event>& events) {\n\tstd::sort(events.begin(), events.end(), [](const Event& a, const Event& b) {\n\t\treturn a.time < b.time;\n\t});\n}\n---\nO(n^2) selection sorting for events is inefficient as the number of events grows. std::sort is O(n log n) and is designed to take advantage of temporal and spatial locality in the cache. This results in much higher performance for time-series or event-based data processing."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortFlagArray(std::vector<int>& a) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < a.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < a.size(); j++) {\n\t\t\tif (a[i] > a[j]) std::swap(a[i], a[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortFlagArray(std::vector<int>& a) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = static_cast<int>(a.size()) - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (a[mid] == 0) {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(a[low++], a[mid++]);\n\t\t} else if (a[mid] == 1) {\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(a[mid], a[high--]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nSorting flags with only three possible values using nested loops is O(n^2). The Dutch National Flag algorithm partitions the flags in O(n) time with a single pass through the array. This improves performance significantly by reducing comparison overhead and writes."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid bubbleSortDouble(std::vector<double>& arr) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < arr.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = 0; j < arr.size() - 1; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (arr[j] > arr[j+1]) std::swap(arr[j], arr[j+1]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid optimizedSort(std::vector<double>& arr) {\n\tstd::sort(arr.begin(), arr.end());\n}\n---\nBubble sort is O(n^2) and is not efficient for floating-point sorting. std::sort is O(n log n) and leverages hardware optimizations for comparisons. This leads to much faster execution, especially for high-precision scientific or financial data."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortColorsSingle(std::vector<int>& nums) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < nums.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < nums.size(); j++) {\n\t\t\tif (nums[i] > nums[j]) std::swap(nums[i], nums[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortColorsSingle(std::vector<int>& nums) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = static_cast<int>(nums.size()) - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (nums[mid] == 0) {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(nums[low++], nums[mid++]);\n\t\t} else if (nums[mid] == 1) {\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(nums[mid], nums[high--]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nThe original O(n^2) algorithm is extremely slow for discrete value sets. The Dutch National Flag algorithm partitions the data in O(n) time in a single pass. This improves performance from quadratic to linear time, which is critical for real-time data processing."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <string>\n\nstruct Price {\n\tint val;\n\tint cents;\n};\n\nvoid sortPrices(std::vector<Price>& p) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < p.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < p.size(); j++) {\n\t\t\tif (p[i].val > p[j].val) std::swap(p[i], p[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstruct Price {\n\tint val;\n\tint cents;\n};\n\nvoid sortPrices(std::vector<Price>& p) {\n\tstd::sort(p.begin(), p.end(), [](const Price& a, const Price& b) {\n\t\tif (a.val != b.val) return a.val < b.val;\n\t\treturn a.cents < b.cents;\n\t});\n}\n---\nManual selection sort is O(n^2) and handles multi-field sorting inefficiently. std::sort is O(n log n) and provides a clean lambda interface for complex sorting logic. This is much faster and more maintainable than manual sorting loops."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortIndices(std::vector<int>& v) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < v.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < v.size(); j++) {\n\t\t\tif (v[i] > v[j]) std::swap(v[i], v[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortIndices(std::vector<int>& v) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = static_cast<int>(v.size()) - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (v[mid] == 0) {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(v[low++], v[mid++]);\n\t\t} else if (v[mid] == 1) {\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(v[mid], v[high--]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nUsing O(n^2) sorting for values in a small discrete range like {0, 1, 2} is suboptimal. The Dutch National Flag algorithm partitions the values in O(n) time with a single pass. This provides the best possible performance for this specific data distribution."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid selectionSortTopK(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < k; ++i) {\n\t\tsize_t min_idx = i;\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < nums.size(); ++j) {\n\t\t\tif (nums[j] < nums[min_idx]) min_idx = j;\n\t\t}\n\t\tstd::swap(nums[i], nums[min_idx]);\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid selectionSortTopK(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n\tif (k < nums.size()) {\n\t\tstd::nth_element(nums.begin(), nums.begin() + k, nums.end());\n\t\tstd::sort(nums.begin(), nums.begin() + k);\n\t}\n}\n---\nManual selection sort is O(n * k), which is inefficient for finding top elements. std::nth_element finds the pivot in O(n) average time, and std::sort orders only the required K elements in O(k log k). This is significantly faster than sorting the entire array or using a manual loop."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortThreeClasses(std::vector<int>& v) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < v.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < v.size(); j++) {\n\t\t\tif (v[i] > v[j]) std::swap(v[i], v[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortThreeClasses(std::vector<int>& v) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = static_cast<int>(v.size()) - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (v[mid] == 0) {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(v[low++], v[mid++]);\n\t\t} else if (v[mid] == 1) {\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(v[mid], v[high--]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nThe original O(n^2) selection sort is inefficient for discrete value sets. The Dutch National Flag algorithm partitions the data in O(n) time using single-pass partitioning. This optimization minimizes memory bandwidth usage and avoids redundant comparisons."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortStable(std::vector<int>& v) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < v.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < v.size(); j++) {\n\t\t\tif (v[i] > v[j]) std::swap(v[i], v[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortStable(std::vector<int>& v) {\n\tstd::stable_sort(v.begin(), v.end());\n}\n---\nSelection sort is O(n^2) and is not stable or cache-efficient. std::stable_sort provides O(n log n) complexity while preserving the relative order of equal elements. This is much faster and uses an optimized mergesort-based approach for performance."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortColorsSinglePassLoop(std::vector<int>& nums) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < nums.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < nums.size(); j++) {\n\t\t\tif (nums[i] > nums[j]) std::swap(nums[i], nums[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortColorsSinglePassLoop(std::vector<int>& nums) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = static_cast<int>(nums.size()) - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (nums[mid] == 0) {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(nums[low++], nums[mid++]);\n\t\t} else if (nums[mid] == 1) {\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(nums[mid], nums[high--]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nThe nested loop approach for sorting three distinct values results in O(n^2) time complexity. The Dutch National Flag algorithm provides a single-pass O(n) solution using three pointers. This significantly reduces the total number of comparisons and writes required."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid bubbleSortIntArray(std::vector<int>& arr) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < arr.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = 0; j < arr.size() - 1; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (arr[j] > arr[j+1]) std::swap(arr[j], arr[j+1]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid optimizedSort(std::vector<int>& arr) {\n\tstd::sort(arr.begin(), arr.end());\n}\n---\nBubble sort is O(n^2), which is extremely slow for large vectors. std::sort utilizes Introsort, which provides O(n log n) performance by combining quicksort, heapsort, and insertion sort. This hybrid approach guarantees consistent and fast sorting performance."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortColorsOptimal(std::vector<int>& nums) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < nums.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < nums.size(); j++) {\n\t\t\tif (nums[i] > nums[j]) std::swap(nums[i], nums[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortColorsOptimal(std::vector<int>& nums) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = static_cast<int>(nums.size()) - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (nums[mid] == 0) {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(nums[low++], nums[mid++]);\n\t\t} else if (nums[mid] == 1) {\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(nums[mid], nums[high--]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nUsing O(n^2) sorting for values restricted to {0, 1, 2} is suboptimal. The Dutch National Flag algorithm partitions the array in O(n) time with a single pass. This provides linear time complexity and minimizes memory operations, making it the most efficient way to sort this data type."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <string>\n\nstruct LogEntry {\n\tlong timestamp;\n\tint level;\n};\n\nvoid sortLogs(std::vector<LogEntry>& logs) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < logs.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < logs.size(); ++j) {\n\t\t\tif (logs[i].level > logs[j].level) {\n\t\t\t\tstd::swap(logs[i], logs[j]);\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstruct LogEntry {\n\tlong timestamp;\n\tint level;\n};\n\nvoid sortLogs(std::vector<LogEntry>& logs) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = static_cast<int>(logs.size()) - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (logs[mid].level == 0) {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(logs[low++], logs[mid++]);\n\t\t} else if (logs[mid].level == 1) {\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(logs[mid], logs[high--]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nThe original implementation used a selection sort with O(n^2) complexity. Since log levels are restricted to a small range (0, 1, 2), the Dutch National Flag algorithm provides a single-pass O(n) solution. This drastically reduces CPU cycles by using three pointers to partition data in-place."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid getLeaderboard(std::vector<int>& scores, int k) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < scores.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = 0; j < scores.size() - 1; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (scores[j] < scores[j+1]) std::swap(scores[j], scores[j+1]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tscores.resize(k);\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid getLeaderboard(std::vector<int>& scores, int k) {\n\tif (k < scores.size()) {\n\t\tstd::partial_sort(scores.begin(), scores.begin() + k, scores.end(), std::greater<int>());\n\t\tscores.resize(k);\n\t}\n}\n---\nThe bubble sort approach takes O(n^2) to rank the entire list even if only the top K are needed. Using std::partial_sort utilizes a heap-based algorithm to achieve O(n log k) complexity. This avoids sorting the lower-ranked scores entirely, saving significant time for large player bases."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nstruct Pixel {\n\tunsigned char r, g, b;\n};\n\nvoid sortPixels(std::vector<Pixel>& p) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 1; i < p.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tPixel key = p[i];\n\t\tint j = i - 1;\n\t\twhile (j >= 0 && p[j].r > key.r) {\n\t\t\tp[j + 1] = p[j];\n\t\t\tj--;\n\t\t}\n\t\tp[j + 1] = key;\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstruct Pixel {\n\tunsigned char r, g, b;\n};\n\nvoid sortPixels(std::vector<Pixel>& p) {\n\tstd::sort(p.begin(), p.end(), [](const Pixel& a, const Pixel& b) {\n\t\treturn a.r < b.r;\n\t});\n}\n---\nInsertion sort is inefficient at O(n^2) for large image buffers. std::sort provides O(n log n) complexity using Introsort, which is much better for large datasets. It also minimizes memory stalls by leveraging a more cache-friendly partitioning strategy."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortTrafficSignals(std::vector<int>& signals) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < signals.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < signals.size(); j++) {\n\t\t\tif (signals[i] > signals[j]) std::swap(signals[i], signals[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortTrafficSignals(std::vector<int>& signals) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = static_cast<int>(signals.size()) - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (signals[mid] == 0) {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(signals[low++], signals[mid++]);\n\t\t} else if (signals[mid] == 1) {\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(signals[mid], signals[high--]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nThe original O(n^2) sort is wasteful for signals typically represented as 0 (Red), 1 (Yellow), 2 (Green). The Dutch National Flag algorithm achieves linear O(n) performance in a single pass. This minimizes the constant overhead associated with comparisons and branch mispredictions."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nstruct Entity {\n\tint id;\n\tint faction; // 0, 1, or 2\n};\n\nvoid sortFactions(std::vector<Entity>& e) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < e.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = 0; j < e.size() - 1; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (e[j].faction > e[j+1].faction) std::swap(e[j], e[j+1]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstruct Entity {\n\tint id;\n\tint faction;\n};\n\nvoid sortFactions(std::vector<Entity>& e) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = static_cast<int>(e.size()) - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (e[mid].faction == 0) {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(e[low++], e[mid++]);\n\t\t} else if (e[mid].faction == 1) {\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(e[mid], e[high--]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nBubble sorting O(n^2) entities by faction is extremely slow as the entity count grows. Since factions are restricted to a range of three, the Dutch National Flag algorithm provides an optimal O(n) partition. This improves game engine performance by reducing the complexity of world-state updates."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortAges(std::vector<int>& ages) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < ages.size(); i++) {\n\t\tsize_t m = i;\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < ages.size(); j++) {\n\t\t\tif (ages[j] < ages[m]) m = j;\n\t\t}\n\t\tstd::swap(ages[i], ages[m]);\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortAges(std::vector<int>& ages) {\n\tstd::sort(ages.begin(), ages.end());\n}\n---\nSelection sort is consistently O(n^2), making it slow for demographic data processing. std::sort uses Introsort, delivering O(n log n) efficiency. Standard library algorithms are also optimized for instruction-level parallelism, making them significantly faster on modern processors."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortBinaryData(std::vector<int>& data) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < data.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < data.size(); j++) {\n\t\t\tif (data[i] > data[j]) std::swap(data[i], data[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortBinaryData(std::vector<int>& data) {\n\tint low = 0, high = static_cast<int>(data.size()) - 1;\n\twhile (low < high) {\n\t\tif (data[low] == 0) low++;\n\t\telse if (data[high] == 1) high--;\n\t\telse std::swap(data[low], data[high]);\n\t}\n}\n---\nUsing O(n^2) selection sort for binary data (0s and 1s) is unnecessary. A two-pointer partitioning approach provides an O(n) single-pass solution. This eliminates redundant comparisons and minimizes writes to memory, which is critical for high-throughput data streams."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <string>\n\nstruct User {\n\tstd::string name;\n\tint role; // 0: Admin, 1: Mod, 2: User\n};\n\nvoid sortUsers(std::vector<User>& users) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < users.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = 0; j < users.size() - 1; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (users[j].role > users[j+1].role) std::swap(users[j], users[j+1]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstruct User {\n\tstd::string name;\n\tint role;\n};\n\nvoid sortUsers(std::vector<User>& users) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = static_cast<int>(users.size()) - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (users[mid].role == 0) {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(users[low++], users[mid++]);\n\t\t} else if (users[mid].role == 1) {\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(users[mid], users[high--]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nSorting user roles with O(n^2) complexity is slow and incurs heavy string move operations. The Dutch National Flag algorithm partitions roles in O(n) time. This single-pass approach minimizes object swaps and is the optimal way to sort based on a small set of enumerated values."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid selectionSortSmall(std::vector<int>& v) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < v.size(); i++) {\n\t\tsize_t min_idx = i;\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < v.size(); j++) {\n\t\t\tif (v[j] < v[min_idx]) min_idx = j;\n\t\t}\n\t\tstd::swap(v[i], v[min_idx]);\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid optimizedSort(std::vector<int>& v) {\n\tstd::sort(v.begin(), v.end());\n}\n---\nSelection sort is inefficient at O(n^2) as it does not benefit from any pre-sorted segments of data. std::sort uses Introsort for O(n log n) efficiency and is highly tuned for cache performance. This ensures the sort scales linearly with data size rather than quadratically."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid topFive(std::vector<double>& vals) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < vals.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = 0; j < vals.size() - 1; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (vals[j] < vals[j+1]) std::swap(vals[j], vals[j+1]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tif (vals.size() > 5) vals.resize(5);\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid topFive(std::vector<double>& vals) {\n\tif (vals.size() > 5) {\n\t\tstd::nth_element(vals.begin(), vals.begin() + 5, vals.end(), std::greater<double>());\n\t\tvals.resize(5);\n\t\tstd::sort(vals.begin(), vals.end(), std::greater<double>());\n\t}\n}\n---\nO(n^2) sorting is overkill for retrieving only five elements. std::nth_element provides the top K elements in O(n) average time without fully sorting the remainder. This optimization is massive for large datasets where most values are discarded."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortSensorData(std::vector<int>& data) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < data.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < data.size(); j++) {\n\t\t\tif (data[i] > data[j]) std::swap(data[i], data[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortSensorData(std::vector<int>& data) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = static_cast<int>(data.size()) - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (data[mid] == 0) {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(data[low++], data[mid++]);\n\t\t} else if (data[mid] == 1) {\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(data[mid], data[high--]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nIf sensor data states are categorical (e.g., Idle, Active, Fault), O(n^2) sorting is suboptimal. The Dutch National Flag algorithm provides a single-pass O(n) sort. This reduces latency in embedded systems by replacing nested loops with a highly efficient linear partitioning logic."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <string>\n\nstruct Transaction {\n\tint type; // 0, 1, 2\n\tdouble amount;\n};\n\nvoid sortTx(std::vector<Transaction>& txs) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < txs.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = 0; j < txs.size() - 1; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (txs[j].type > txs[j+1].type) std::swap(txs[j], txs[j+1]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstruct Transaction {\n\tint type;\n\tdouble amount;\n};\n\nvoid sortTx(std::vector<Transaction>& txs) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = static_cast<int>(txs.size()) - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (txs[mid].type == 0) {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(txs[low++], txs[mid++]);\n\t\t} else if (txs[mid].type == 1) {\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(txs[mid], txs[high--]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nBubble sorting transactions is O(n^2) and very slow for high-frequency financial data. Since transaction types are limited to three categories, the Dutch National Flag algorithm offers an O(n) solution. This single-pass partitioning minimizes cache misses and improves overall transaction throughput."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid slowPartialSort(std::vector<int>& v, int k) {\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < k; ++i) {\n\t\tint min_val = i;\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < v.size(); ++j) {\n\t\t\tif (v[j] < v[min_val]) min_val = j;\n\t\t}\n\t\tstd::swap(v[i], v[min_val]);\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid fastPartialSort(std::vector<int>& v, int k) {\n\tif (k < v.size()) {\n\t\tstd::partial_sort(v.begin(), v.begin() + k, v.end());\n\t}\n}\n---\nManual selection sort to extract K elements is O(n*k). For large K, this approach is extremely inefficient compared to std::partial_sort, which operates in O(n log k). The optimized version uses a heap structure to keep track of the smallest K elements, minimizing comparisons across the remainder of the array."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortPackets(std::vector<int>& packets) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 1; i < packets.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tint val = packets[i];\n\t\tint j = i - 1;\n\t\twhile (j >= 0 && packets[j] > val) {\n\t\t\tpackets[j + 1] = packets[j];\n\t\t\tj--;\n\t\t}\n\t\tpackets[j + 1] = val;\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortPackets(std::vector<int>& packets) {\n\tstd::sort(packets.begin(), packets.end());\n}\n---\nInsertion sort is O(n^2) on average, making it a bottleneck for network packet processing. std::sort uses Introsort to guarantee O(n log n) performance even in the worst-case scenario. This switch leads to significantly more predictable and efficient packet reordering."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortThreePriorities(std::vector<int>& p) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < p.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < p.size(); j++) {\n\t\t\tif (p[i] > p[j]) std::swap(p[i], p[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortThreePriorities(std::vector<int>& p) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = static_cast<int>(p.size()) - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (p[mid] == 0) {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(p[low++], p[mid++]);\n\t\t} else if (p[mid] == 1) {\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(p[mid], p[high--]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nNested loops for sorting priorities is O(n^2), causing performance degradation as task counts increase. The Dutch National Flag algorithm provides an O(n) solution specifically for three-value datasets. This single-pass partitioning is the optimal approach for categorizing tasks by priority levels."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nstruct Score {\n\tint points;\n\tint player_id;\n};\n\nvoid sortByScore(std::vector<Score>& s) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < s.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = 0; j < s.size() - 1; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (s[j].points > s[j+1].points) std::swap(s[j], s[j+1]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstruct Score {\n\tint points;\n\tint player_id;\n};\n\nvoid sortByScore(std::vector<Score>& s) {\n\tstd::sort(s.begin(), s.end(), [](const Score& a, const Score& b) {\n\t\treturn a.points < b.points;\n\t});\n}\n---\nBubble sorting game scores is O(n^2), which is inefficient for leaderboard processing. std::sort delivers O(n log n) performance using a hybrid algorithm. This minimizes CPU load and improves frame rate stability by reducing the time spent in object management and comparisons."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortStockStates(std::vector<int>& s) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < s.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < s.size(); j++) {\n\t\t\tif (s[i] > s[j]) std::swap(s[i], s[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortStockStates(std::vector<int>& s) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = static_cast<int>(s.size()) - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (s[mid] == 0) {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(s[low++], s[mid++]);\n\t\t} else if (s[mid] == 1) {\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(s[mid], s[high--]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nThe original O(n^2) approach is too slow for real-time stock state sorting (e.g., Falling, Stable, Rising). The Dutch National Flag algorithm provides a single-pass O(n) solution. This linear complexity ensures the algorithm scales efficiently with the volume of market data updates."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid manualPartialSort(std::vector<int>& v, int k) {\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < k; i++) {\n\t\tint min_idx = i;\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < v.size(); j++) {\n\t\t\tif (v[j] < v[min_idx]) min_idx = j;\n\t\t}\n\t\tstd::swap(v[i], v[min_idx]);\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid fastPartialSort(std::vector<int>& v, int k) {\n\tif (k < v.size()) {\n\t\tstd::partial_sort(v.begin(), v.begin() + k, v.end());\n\t}\n}\n---\nUsing selection sort for a partial ordering results in O(n*k) time complexity. std::partial_sort is O(n log k) and is designed specifically for these scenarios. It uses a max-heap to find the k smallest elements, which is vastly more efficient for large input vectors."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortNearlySortedArray(std::vector<int>& v) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 1; i < v.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tint temp = v[i];\n\t\tint j = i - 1;\n\t\twhile (j >= 0 && v[j] > temp) {\n\t\t\tv[j + 1] = v[j];\n\t\t\tj--;\n\t\t}\n\t\tv[j + 1] = temp;\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortNearlySortedArray(std::vector<int>& v) {\n\tstd::sort(v.begin(), v.end());\n}\n---\nWhile insertion sort is O(n) for strictly sorted arrays, it remains O(n^2) on average. std::sort uses Introsort for O(n log n) efficiency and includes internal insertion sort optimizations for small partitions. This provides a better balance of performance and worst-case protection for generally sorted data."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortCategory(std::vector<int>& c) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < c.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < c.size(); j++) {\n\t\t\tif (c[i] > c[j]) std::swap(c[i], c[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortCategory(std::vector<int>& c) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = static_cast<int>(c.size()) - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (c[mid] == 0) {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(c[low++], c[mid++]);\n\t\t} else if (c[mid] == 1) {\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(c[mid], c[high--]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nIf categories are encoded as 0, 1, and 2, the original O(n^2) sort is highly inefficient. The Dutch National Flag algorithm provides an O(n) single-pass sorting solution. This minimizes comparison count and maximizes memory throughput by avoiding nested loop overhead."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <string>\n\nstruct File {\n\tstd::string name;\n\tint type; // 0, 1, 2\n};\n\nvoid sortFiles(std::vector<File>& f) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < f.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = 0; j < f.size() - 1; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (f[j].type > f[j+1].type) std::swap(f[j], f[j+1]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstruct File {\n\tstd::string name;\n\tint type;\n};\n\nvoid sortFiles(std::vector<File>& f) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = static_cast<int>(f.size()) - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (f[mid].type == 0) {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(f[low++], f[mid++]);\n\t\t} else if (f[mid].type == 1) {\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(f[mid], f[high--]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nBubble sorting files by type is O(n^2) and incurs high swap costs for structs containing strings. The Dutch National Flag algorithm provides a linear O(n) partitioning strategy. This minimizes the number of string move operations and provides significant speedups for large file systems."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid selectionSortTopResults(std::vector<int>& r, int k) {\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < k; ++i) {\n\t\tint best = i;\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < r.size(); ++j) {\n\t\t\tif (r[j] > r[best]) best = j;\n\t\t}\n\t\tstd::swap(r[i], r[best]);\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid selectionSortTopResults(std::vector<int>& r, int k) {\n\tif (k < r.size()) {\n\t\tstd::nth_element(r.begin(), r.begin() + k, r.end(), std::greater<int>());\n\t\tstd::sort(r.begin(), r.begin() + k, std::greater<int>());\n\t}\n}\n---\nManual selection sort is O(n*k) for retrieving the top K results. std::nth_element achieves O(n) average performance to find the top K elements, and std::sort is only applied to the small K subset. This combination is much faster for large result sets where only a few are displayed."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortDNASequence(std::vector<int>& seq) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 1; i < seq.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tint base = seq[i];\n\t\tint j = i - 1;\n\t\twhile (j >= 0 && seq[j] > base) {\n\t\t\tseq[j + 1] = seq[j];\n\t\t\tj--;\n\t\t}\n\t\tseq[j + 1] = base;\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortDNASequence(std::vector<int>& seq) {\n\tstd::sort(seq.begin(), seq.end());\n}\n---\nInsertion sort is O(n^2) on average and is slow for the long sequences common in DNA analysis. std::sort provides O(n log n) efficiency and is much more suitable for large genomic datasets. It reduces the processing time for base reordering by leveraging a more advanced partitioning strategy."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortTraffic(std::vector<int>& t) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < t.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < t.size(); j++) {\n\t\t\tif (t[i] > t[j]) std::swap(t[i], t[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortTraffic(std::vector<int>& t) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = static_cast<int>(t.size()) - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (t[mid] == 0) {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(t[low++], t[mid++]);\n\t\t} else if (t[mid] == 1) {\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(t[mid], t[high--]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nUsing O(n^2) selection sort for categorical traffic data (e.g., Car, Truck, Bike) is extremely inefficient. The Dutch National Flag algorithm provides an O(n) single-pass partitioning solution. This linear complexity improves the speed of high-frequency traffic simulation and monitoring systems."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nstruct Task {\n\tint id;\n\tint urgency; // 0, 1, 2\n};\n\nvoid sortUrgency(std::vector<Task>& t) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < t.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = 0; j < t.size() - 1; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (t[j].urgency > t[j+1].urgency) std::swap(t[j], t[j+1]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstruct Task {\n\tint id;\n\tint urgency;\n};\n\nvoid sortUrgency(std::vector<Task>& t) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = static_cast<int>(t.size()) - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (t[mid].urgency == 0) {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(t[low++], t[mid++]);\n\t\t} else if (t[mid].urgency == 1) {\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(t[mid], t[high--]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nO(n^2) bubble sort on tasks is slow for job scheduling systems. Since urgency is categorical, the Dutch National Flag algorithm provides an optimal O(n) partitioning logic. This linear approach significantly reduces scheduling latency and improves system responsiveness."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid topTen(std::vector<int>& v) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < v.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = 0; j < v.size() - 1; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (v[j] < v[j+1]) std::swap(v[j], v[j+1]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tif (v.size() > 10) v.resize(10);\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid topTen(std::vector<int>& v) {\n\tif (v.size() > 10) {\n\t\tstd::nth_element(v.begin(), v.begin() + 10, v.end(), std::greater<int>());\n\t\tv.resize(10);\n\t\tstd::sort(v.begin(), v.end(), std::greater<int>());\n\t}\n}\n---\nFull O(n^2) bubble sorting to find only ten items is a massive bottleneck. std::nth_element finds the top elements in O(n) average time. This avoids wasting CPU cycles on fully ordering the thousands of elements that are eventually discarded."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortBinaryArray(std::vector<int>& a) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < a.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < a.size(); j++) {\n\t\t\tif (a[i] > a[j]) std::swap(a[i], a[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortBinaryArray(std::vector<int>& a) {\n\tint low = 0, high = static_cast<int>(a.size()) - 1;\n\twhile (low < high) {\n\t\tif (a[low] == 0) low++;\n\t\telse if (a[high] == 1) high--;\n\t\telse std::swap(a[low], a[high]);\n\t}\n}\n---\nNested loops for binary value partitioning result in O(n^2) time complexity. A single-pass O(n) solution using two pointers is much more efficient. This optimization minimizes memory reads and eliminates unnecessary comparisons for boolean or state-based arrays."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nstruct Player {\n\tint level;\n\tint score;\n};\n\nvoid sortPlayers(std::vector<Player>& p) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < p.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = 0; j < p.size() - 1; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (p[j].level > p[j+1].level) std::swap(p[j], p[j+1]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstruct Player {\n\tint level;\n\tint score;\n};\n\nvoid sortPlayers(std::vector<Player>& p) {\n\tstd::sort(p.begin(), p.end(), [](const Player& a, const Player& b) {\n\t\tif (a.level != b.level) return a.level < b.level;\n\t\treturn a.score < b.score;\n\t});\n}\n---\nBubble sorting player records is O(n^2) and very slow for large player lists. std::sort provides O(n log n) efficiency and a clean way to handle multi-field comparison logic. This switch drastically reduces the time needed for matchmaking or ranking updates in game systems."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortStateCodes(std::vector<int>& s) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < s.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < s.size(); j++) {\n\t\t\tif (s[i] > s[j]) std::swap(s[i], s[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortStateCodes(std::vector<int>& s) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = static_cast<int>(s.size()) - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (s[mid] == 0) {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(s[low++], s[mid++]);\n\t\t} else if (s[mid] == 1) {\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(s[mid], s[high--]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nSelection sorting state codes restricted to 0, 1, and 2 is extremely inefficient at O(n^2). The Dutch National Flag algorithm provides an O(n) single-pass partitioning strategy. This linear approach provides the best possible performance for categorizing discrete state values."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid manualPartialOrder(std::vector<int>& v, int k) {\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < k; ++i) {\n\t\tsize_t idx = i;\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < v.size(); ++j) {\n\t\t\tif (v[j] < v[idx]) idx = j;\n\t\t}\n\t\tstd::swap(v[i], v[idx]);\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid fastPartialOrder(std::vector<int>& v, int k) {\n\tif (k < v.size()) {\n\t\tstd::partial_sort(v.begin(), v.begin() + k, v.end());\n\t}\n}\n---\nManual selection sort to extract K elements is O(n*k), which scales poorly for large datasets. std::partial_sort is O(n log k) and is highly optimized for this exact use case. It uses a heap to maintain the top K elements, making it the superior choice for partial sorting tasks."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortSensorArray(std::vector<int>& s) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 1; i < s.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tint k = s[i];\n\t\tint j = i - 1;\n\t\twhile (j >= 0 && s[j] > k) {\n\t\t\ts[j+1] = s[j];\n\t\t\tj--;\n\t\t}\n\t\ts[j+1] = k;\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortSensorArray(std::vector<int>& s) {\n\tstd::sort(s.begin(), s.end());\n}\n---\nInsertion sort is O(n^2) on average and can lead to performance issues in sensor data processing. std::sort provides O(n log n) efficiency and handles various data distributions gracefully. This ensures consistent and fast sorting for high-frequency signal processing."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortTypes(std::vector<int>& t) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < t.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < t.size(); j++) {\n\t\t\tif (t[i] > t[j]) std::swap(t[i], t[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortTypes(std::vector<int>& t) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = static_cast<int>(t.size()) - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (t[mid] == 0) {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(t[low++], t[mid++]);\n\t\t} else if (t[mid] == 1) {\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(t[mid], t[high--]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nSorting categorical types with O(n^2) selection sort is very slow. The Dutch National Flag algorithm provides a single-pass O(n) solution using three pointers. This optimization is ideal for categorization tasks where values are restricted to a small integer range."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nstruct Product {\n\tint category;\n\tint price;\n};\n\nvoid sortCategories(std::vector<Product>& p) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < p.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = 0; j < p.size() - 1; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (p[j].category > p[j+1].category) std::swap(p[j], p[j+1]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstruct Product {\n\tint category;\n\tint price;\n};\n\nvoid sortCategories(std::vector<Product>& p) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = static_cast<int>(p.size()) - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (p[mid].category == 0) {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(p[low++], p[mid++]);\n\t\t} else if (p[mid].category == 1) {\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(p[mid], p[high--]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nBubble sorting products by category is O(n^2) and incurs high object movement overhead. The Dutch National Flag algorithm provides an O(n) single-pass partition. This linear time approach is the most efficient way to group products when there are only three distinct categories."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid manualPartialSortScores(std::vector<int>& s, int k) {\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < k; ++i) {\n\t\tint min_idx = i;\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < s.size(); ++j) {\n\t\t\tif (s[j] < s[min_idx]) min_idx = j;\n\t\t}\n\t\tstd::swap(s[i], s[min_idx]);\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid fastPartialSortScores(std::vector<int>& s, int k) {\n\tif (k < s.size()) {\n\t\tstd::partial_sort(s.begin(), s.begin() + k, s.end());\n\t}\n}\n---\nManual selection sort for a partial ordering is O(n*k), which is slow for large scoreboards. std::partial_sort is O(n log k) and is specifically tuned for retrieving the smallest K elements efficiently. This optimization significantly reduces the constant time factors for large input datasets."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortMeasurementArray(std::vector<double>& m) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 1; i < m.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tdouble val = m[i];\n\t\tint j = i - 1;\n\t\twhile (j >= 0 && m[j] > val) {\n\t\t\tm[j+1] = m[j];\n\t\t\tj--;\n\t\t}\n\t\tm[j+1] = val;\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortMeasurementArray(std::vector<double>& m) {\n\tstd::sort(m.begin(), m.end());\n}\n---\nInsertion sort is O(n^2) and is not suitable for high-precision measurement data processing. std::sort provides O(n log n) efficiency and is optimized for floating-point comparisons. This switch ensures that the algorithm remains performant even as the sample count grows."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortDiscreteValues(std::vector<int>& d) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < d.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < d.size(); j++) {\n\t\t\tif (d[i] > d[j]) std::swap(d[i], d[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortDiscreteValues(std::vector<int>& d) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = static_cast<int>(d.size()) - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (d[mid] == 0) {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(d[low++], d[mid++]);\n\t\t} else if (d[mid] == 1) {\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(d[mid], d[high--]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nSelection sorting values restricted to {0, 1, 2} is extremely inefficient at O(n^2). The Dutch National Flag algorithm provides an O(n) single-pass partitioning strategy. This approach is the optimal choice for sorting discrete data sets with minimal comparison and write cycles."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nstruct Account {\n\tint type; // 0, 1, 2\n\tint id;\n};\n\nvoid sortAccounts(std::vector<Account>& a) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < a.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = 0; j < a.size() - 1; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (a[j].type > a[j+1].type) std::swap(a[j], a[j+1]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstruct Account {\n\tint type;\n\tint id;\n};\n\nvoid sortAccounts(std::vector<Account>& a) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = static_cast<int>(a.size()) - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (a[mid].type == 0) {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(a[low++], a[mid++]);\n\t\t} else if (a[mid].type == 1) {\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(a[mid], a[high--]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nBubble sorting accounts by type is O(n^2) and incurs high object movement overhead. The Dutch National Flag algorithm provides an O(n) single-pass partition. This linear approach is the most efficient way to categorize accounts when there are only three distinct types."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid manualTopK(std::vector<int>& v, int k) {\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < k; ++i) {\n\t\tsize_t best = i;\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < v.size(); ++j) {\n\t\t\tif (v[j] > v[best]) best = j;\n\t\t}\n\t\tstd::swap(v[i], v[best]);\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid fastTopK(std::vector<int>& v, int k) {\n\tif (k < v.size()) {\n\t\tstd::nth_element(v.begin(), v.begin() + k, v.end(), std::greater<int>());\n\t\tstd::sort(v.begin(), v.begin() + k, std::greater<int>());\n\t}\n}\n---\nManual selection sort is O(n*k) for finding the top K items. std::nth_element achieve O(n) average performance to isolate the top K elements, and std::sort is only needed for that small subset. This strategy is much faster for large lists where only a few items are needed."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortReadingArray(std::vector<int>& r) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 1; i < r.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tint val = r[i];\n\t\tint j = i - 1;\n\t\twhile (j >= 0 && r[j] > val) {\n\t\t\tr[j+1] = r[j];\n\t\t\tj--;\n\t\t}\n\t\tr[j+1] = val;\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortReadingArray(std::vector<int>& r) {\n\tstd::sort(r.begin(), r.end());\n}\n---\nInsertion sort is O(n^2) on average and can be a significant bottleneck for reading processing. std::sort provides O(n log n) efficiency and handles various data distributions effectively. This ensures that the algorithm scales well as the size of the reading array increases."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortThreeStatesArray(std::vector<int>& a) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < a.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < a.size(); j++) {\n\t\t\tif (a[i] > a[j]) std::swap(a[i], a[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortThreeStatesArray(std::vector<int>& a) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = static_cast<int>(a.size()) - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (a[mid] == 0) {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(a[low++], a[mid++]);\n\t\t} else if (a[mid] == 1) {\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(a[mid], a[high--]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nSelection sorting values restricted to {0, 1, 2} is extremely inefficient at O(n^2). The Dutch National Flag algorithm provides an O(n) single-pass partitioning strategy. This linear approach provides the best possible performance for categorizing discrete array states."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nstruct Item {\n\tint priority; // 0, 1, 2\n\tint id;\n};\n\nvoid sortItemsByPriority(std::vector<Item>& i) {\n\tfor (size_t x = 0; x < i.size(); x++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t y = 0; y < i.size() - 1; y++) {\n\t\t\tif (i[y].priority > i[y+1].priority) std::swap(i[y], i[y+1]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstruct Item {\n\tint priority;\n\tint id;\n};\n\nvoid sortItemsByPriority(std::vector<Item>& i) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = static_cast<int>(i.size()) - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (i[mid].priority == 0) {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(i[low++], i[mid++]);\n\t\t} else if (i[mid].priority == 1) {\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(i[mid], i[high--]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nBubble sorting items by priority is O(n^2) and incurs high object movement overhead. The Dutch National Flag algorithm provides an O(n) single-pass partition. This linear approach is the most efficient way to group items when there are only three distinct priority levels."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid manualPartialScoreSort(std::vector<int>& scores, int k) {\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < k; ++i) {\n\t\tint min_idx = i;\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < scores.size(); ++j) {\n\t\t\tif (scores[j] < scores[min_idx]) min_idx = j;\n\t\t}\n\t\tstd::swap(scores[i], scores[min_idx]);\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid fastPartialScoreSort(std::vector<int>& scores, int k) {\n\tif (k < scores.size()) {\n\t\tstd::partial_sort(scores.begin(), scores.begin() + k, scores.end());\n\t}\n}\n---\nManual selection sort for a partial ordering is O(n*k), which is slow for large score arrays. std::partial_sort is O(n log k) and is specifically tuned for retrieving the smallest K elements efficiently. This optimization significantly reduces the constant time factors for large input datasets."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortMeasurementList(std::vector<double>& v) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 1; i < v.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tdouble val = v[i];\n\t\tint j = i - 1;\n\t\twhile (j >= 0 && v[j] > val) {\n\t\t\tv[j+1] = v[j];\n\t\t\tj--;\n\t\t}\n\t\tv[j+1] = val;\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortMeasurementList(std::vector<double>& v) {\n\tstd::sort(v.begin(), v.end());\n}\n---\nInsertion sort is O(n^2) on average and is not suitable for large measurement list processing. std::sort provides O(n log n) efficiency and handles various data distributions gracefully. This switch ensures that the algorithm remains performant even as the sample count grows."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortCategoricalArray(std::vector<int>& arr) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < arr.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < arr.size(); j++) {\n\t\t\tif (arr[i] > arr[j]) std::swap(arr[i], arr[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortCategoricalArray(std::vector<int>& arr) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = static_cast<int>(arr.size()) - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (arr[mid] == 0) {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(arr[low++], arr[mid++]);\n\t\t} else if (arr[mid] == 1) {\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(arr[mid], arr[high--]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nSelection sorting categorical values restricted to {0, 1, 2} is extremely inefficient at O(n^2). The Dutch National Flag algorithm provides an O(n) single-pass partitioning strategy. This approach is the optimal choice for sorting categorical arrays with minimal comparison cycles."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nstruct TransactionRecord {\n\tint status; // 0, 1, 2\n\tint amount;\n};\n\nvoid sortTransactions(std::vector<TransactionRecord>& t) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < t.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = 0; j < t.size() - 1; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (t[j].status > t[j+1].status) std::swap(t[j], t[j+1]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstruct TransactionRecord {\n\tint status;\n\tint amount;\n};\n\nvoid sortTransactions(std::vector<TransactionRecord>& t) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = static_cast<int>(t.size()) - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (t[mid].status == 0) {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(t[low++], t[mid++]);\n\t\t} else if (t[mid].status == 1) {\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(t[mid], t[high--]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nBubble sorting transaction status is O(n^2) and incurs high object movement overhead. The Dutch National Flag algorithm provides an O(n) single-pass partition. This linear approach is the most efficient way to group records by status when there are only three distinct states."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid manualTopKResults(std::vector<int>& v, int k) {\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < k; ++i) {\n\t\tsize_t best_idx = i;\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < v.size(); ++j) {\n\t\t\tif (v[j] > v[best_idx]) best_idx = j;\n\t\t}\n\t\tstd::swap(v[i], v[best_idx]);\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid fastTopKResults(std::vector<int>& v, int k) {\n\tif (k < v.size()) {\n\t\tstd::nth_element(v.begin(), v.begin() + k, v.end(), std::greater<int>());\n\t\tstd::sort(v.begin(), v.begin() + k, std::greater<int>());\n\t}\n}\n---\nManual selection sort is O(n*k) for finding the top K elements. std::nth_element achieve O(n) average performance to isolate those elements, and std::sort is only applied to that small subset. This strategy is much faster for large lists where only a few items are needed."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortSignalArray(std::vector<int>& s) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 1; i < s.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tint val = s[i];\n\t\tint j = i - 1;\n\t\twhile (j >= 0 && s[j] > val) {\n\t\t\ts[j+1] = s[j];\n\t\t\tj--;\n\t\t}\n\t\ts[j+1] = val;\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortSignalArray(std::vector<int>& s) {\n\tstd::sort(s.begin(), s.end());\n}\n---\nInsertion sort is O(n^2) on average and can be a significant bottleneck for signal data processing. std::sort provides O(n log n) efficiency and handles various data distributions gracefully. This switch ensures that the algorithm remains performant even as the signal array size increases."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortThreeClassesArray(std::vector<int>& v) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < v.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < v.size(); j++) {\n\t\t\tif (v[i] > v[j]) std::swap(v[i], v[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortThreeClassesArray(std::vector<int>& v) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = static_cast<int>(v.size()) - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (v[mid] == 0) {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(v[low++], v[mid++]);\n\t\t} else if (v[mid] == 1) {\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(v[mid], v[high--]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nSelection sorting values restricted to {0, 1, 2} is extremely inefficient at O(n^2). The Dutch National Flag algorithm provides an O(n) single-pass partitioning strategy. This approach is the optimal choice for sorting categorical arrays with minimal comparison cycles."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nstruct DataEntry {\n\tint level; // 0, 1, 2\n\tint id;\n};\n\nvoid sortDataEntries(std::vector<DataEntry>& d) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < d.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = 0; j < d.size() - 1; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (d[j].level > d[j+1].level) std::swap(d[j], d[j+1]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstruct DataEntry {\n\tint level;\n\tint id;\n};\n\nvoid sortDataEntries(std::vector<DataEntry>& d) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = static_cast<int>(d.size()) - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (d[mid].level == 0) {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(d[low++], d[mid++]);\n\t\t} else if (d[mid].level == 1) {\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(d[mid], d[high--]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nBubble sorting data entries by level is O(n^2) and incurs high object movement overhead. The Dutch National Flag algorithm provides an O(n) single-pass partition. This linear approach is the most efficient way to group records by level when there are only three distinct states."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid manualTopKScores(std::vector<int>& v, int k) {\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < k; ++i) {\n\t\tsize_t min_idx = i;\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < v.size(); ++j) {\n\t\t\tif (v[j] < v[min_idx]) min_idx = j;\n\t\t}\n\t\tstd::swap(v[i], v[min_idx]);\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid fastTopKScores(std::vector<int>& v, int k) {\n\tif (k < v.size()) {\n\t\tstd::nth_element(v.begin(), v.begin() + k, v.end());\n\t\tstd::sort(v.begin(), v.begin() + k);\n\t}\n}\n---\nManual selection sort is O(n*k) for finding the top K scores. std::nth_element achieve O(n) average performance to isolate those scores, and std::sort is only applied to that small subset. This strategy is much faster for large lists where only a few scores are needed."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nstruct Patient {\n\tint triage; // 0: Critical, 1: Urgent, 2: Stable\n\tint id;\n};\n\nvoid triageSort(std::vector<Patient>& p) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < p.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < p.size(); j++) {\n\t\t\tif (p[i].triage > p[j].triage) std::swap(p[i], p[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstruct Patient {\n\tint triage;\n\tint id;\n};\n\nvoid triageSort(std::vector<Patient>& p) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = static_cast<int>(p.size()) - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (p[mid].triage == 0) std::swap(p[low++], p[mid++]);\n\t\telse if (p[mid].triage == 1) mid++;\n\t\telse std::swap(p[mid], p[high--]);\n\t}\n}\n---\nThe original O(n^2) selection sort is too slow for emergency room systems. Since triage levels are limited to three categories, the Dutch National Flag algorithm provides an O(n) single-pass solution, ensuring deterministic performance regardless of patient volume."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortShippingSpeed(std::vector<int>& s) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < s.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = 0; j < s.size() - 1; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (s[j] > s[j+1]) std::swap(s[j], s[j+1]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortShippingSpeed(std::vector<int>& s) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = static_cast<int>(s.size()) - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (s[mid] == 0) std::swap(s[low++], s[mid++]);\n\t\telse if (s[mid] == 1) mid++;\n\t\telse std::swap(s[mid], s[high--]);\n\t}\n}\n---\nBubble sorting logistics data (Economy, Standard, Express) is O(n^2). The Dutch National Flag algorithm partitions these in O(n). This minimizes memory bandwidth usage and speeds up order fulfillment processing."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid getMedian(std::vector<int>& v) {\n\tstd::sort(v.begin(), v.end());\n\tint median = v[v.size()/2];\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid getMedian(std::vector<int>& v) {\n\tif (v.empty()) return;\n\tsize_t mid = v.size() / 2;\n\tstd::nth_element(v.begin(), v.begin() + mid, v.end());\n\tint median = v[mid];\n}\n---\nSorting an entire array O(n log n) just to find the median is inefficient. std::nth_element uses a selection algorithm to find the middle element in O(n) average time, significantly reducing computation for large statistical datasets."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <string>\n\nbool hasUser(const std::vector[std::string](https://www.google.com/search?q=std::string)& users, const https://www.google.com/search?q=std::string& target) {\n\tfor (const auto& user : users) {\n\t\tif (user == target) return true;\n\t}\n\treturn false;\n}", "output": "#include <unordered_set>\n#include <string>\n\n// Change data structure from vector to unordered_set\nbool hasUser(const std::unordered_set[std::string](https://www.google.com/search?q=std::string)& users, const https://www.google.com/search?q=std::string& target) {\n\treturn users.find(target) != users.end();\n}\n---\nLinear search in a vector is O(n). Switching to an unordered_set changes the lookup to O(1) average time. This is critical for authentication systems where user lists can grow to millions of entries."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortBatteries(std::vector<int>& b) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < b.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < b.size(); j++) {\n\t\t\tif (b[i] > b[j]) std::swap(b[i], b[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortBatteries(std::vector<int>& b) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = static_cast<int>(b.size()) - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (b[mid] == 0) std::swap(b[low++], mid++);\n\t\telse if (b[mid] == 1) mid++;\n\t\telse std::swap(b[mid], b[high--]);\n\t}\n}\n---\nFor battery states (Dead, Charging, Full), O(n^2) sorting is overkill. A single-pass partitioning algorithm (DNF) sorts the states in O(n), reducing power consumption in the monitoring device by minimizing CPU cycles."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nstruct MemoryBlock {\n\tint status; // 0: Free, 1: Reserved, 2: Fragmented\n\tint size;\n};\n\nvoid sortBlocks(std::vector<MemoryBlock>& m) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < m.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = 0; j < m.size() - 1; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (m[j].status > m[j+1].status) std::swap(m[j], m[j+1]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstruct MemoryBlock {\n\tint status;\n\tint size;\n};\n\nvoid sortBlocks(std::vector<MemoryBlock>& m) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = static_cast<int>(m.size()) - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (m[mid].status == 0) std::swap(m[low++], m[mid++]);\n\t\telse if (m[mid].status == 1) mid++;\n\t\telse std::swap(m[mid], m[high--]);\n\t}\n}\n---\nSorting memory blocks in O(n^2) during garbage collection increases system latency. An O(n) partition using DNF ensures memory management remains fast and responsive, which is vital for real-time operating systems."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortBinarySignals(std::vector<int>& s) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 1; i < s.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tint val = s[i];\n\t\tint j = i - 1;\n\t\twhile (j >= 0 && s[j] > val) {\n\t\t\ts[j+1] = s[j];\n\t\t\tj--;\n\t\t}\n\t\ts[j+1] = val;\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortBinarySignals(std::vector<int>& s) {\n\tint left = 0, right = static_cast<int>(s.size()) - 1;\n\twhile (left < right) {\n\t\twhile (left < right && s[left] == 0) left++;\n\t\twhile (left < right && s[right] == 1) right--;\n\t\tif (left < right) {\n\t\t\ts[left++] = 0;\n\t\t\ts[right--] = 1;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nInsertion sort is O(n^2) even for binary data. A two-pointer partitioning approach sorts 0s and 1s in O(n) time with minimal writes. This is ideal for signal processing where throughput is the primary constraint."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortWeather(std::vector<int>& w) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < w.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < w.size(); j++) {\n\t\t\tif (w[i] > w[j]) std::swap(w[i], w[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortWeather(std::vector<int>& w) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = static_cast<int>(w.size()) - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (w[mid] == 0) std::swap(w[low++], w[mid++]);\n\t\telse if (w[mid] == 1) mid++;\n\t\telse std::swap(w[mid], w[high--]);\n\t}\n}\n---\nClassifying weather conditions (Sunny, Cloudy, Rainy) using O(n^2) sort is inefficient for historical data analysis. The Dutch National Flag algorithm provides an O(n) solution, which allows for faster processing of large-scale climate datasets."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nstruct Product {\n\tint id;\n\tint rating; // 1 to 5\n};\n\nvoid sortRatings(std::vector<Product>& p) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < p.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = 0; j < p.size() - 1; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (p[j].rating < p[j+1].rating) std::swap(p[j], p[j+1]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstruct Product {\n\tint id;\n\tint rating;\n};\n\nvoid sortRatings(std::vector<Product>& p) {\n\tstd::sort(p.begin(), p.end(), [](const Product& a, const Product& b) {\n\t\treturn a.rating > b.rating;\n\t});\n}\n---\nBubble sorting products by rating is O(n^2). Switching to std::sort provides O(n log n) complexity. For e-commerce sites with thousands of items, this reduction in complexity ensures that user-facing catalog pages load significantly faster."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid findTop3(std::vector<int>& scores) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < scores.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = 0; j < scores.size() - 1; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (scores[j] < scores[j+1]) std::swap(scores[j], scores[j+1]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tscores.resize(3);\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid findTop3(std::vector<int>& scores) {\n\tif (scores.size() > 3) {\n\t\tstd::partial_sort(scores.begin(), scores.begin() + 3, scores.end(), std::greater<int>());\n\t\tscores.resize(3);\n\t}\n}\n---\nSorting the entire array to find the top 3 elements is O(n log n) or O(n^2) here. std::partial_sort uses a heap to find the top K in O(n log k). This is far more efficient when K is very small compared to N."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nstruct Enemy {\n\tint type; // 0: Minion, 1: Elite, 2: Boss\n\tfloat health;\n};\n\nvoid sortEnemies(std::vector<Enemy>& e) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < e.size(); i++) {\n\t\tsize_t min = i;\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < e.size(); j++) {\n\t\t\tif (e[j].type < e[min].type) min = j;\n\t\t}\n\t\tstd::swap(e[i], e[min]);\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstruct Enemy {\n\tint type;\n\tfloat health;\n};\n\nvoid sortEnemies(std::vector<Enemy>& e) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = static_cast<int>(e.size()) - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (e[mid].type == 0) std::swap(e[low++], e[mid++]);\n\t\telse if (e[mid].type == 1) mid++;\n\t\telse std::swap(e[mid], e[high--]);\n\t}\n}\n---\nIn a game loop, O(n^2) selection sort can cause frame drops. Using the O(n) Dutch National Flag algorithm to sort enemies by type ensures the engine maintains a high frame rate even during large-scale battles."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortAisles(std::vector<int>& a) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < a.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < a.size(); j++) {\n\t\t\tif (a[i] > a[j]) std::swap(a[i], a[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortAisles(std::vector<int>& a) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = static_cast<int>(a.size()) - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (a[mid] == 0) std::swap(a[low++], a[mid++]);\n\t\telse if (a[mid] == 1) mid++;\n\t\telse std::swap(a[mid], a[high--]);\n\t}\n}\n---\nSorting supermarket aisle categories (Perishables, Frozen, Dry) in O(n^2) is inefficient for warehouse routing. The O(n) DNF algorithm optimizes the pathing logic for robots, reducing task completion time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <string>\n\nstruct Task {\n\tint priority;\n\thttps://www.google.com/search?q=std::string desc;\n};\n\nvoid sortTasks(std::vector<Task>& t) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 1; i < t.size(); i++) {\n\t\tTask key = t[i];\n\t\tint j = i - 1;\n\t\twhile (j >= 0 && t[j].priority > key.priority) {\n\t\t\tt[j+1] = t[j];\n\t\t\tj--;\n\t\t}\n\t\tt[j+1] = key;\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstruct Task {\n\tint priority;\n\thttps://www.google.com/search?q=std::string desc;\n};\n\nvoid sortTasks(std::vector<Task>& t) {\n\tstd::sort(t.begin(), t.end(), [](const Task& a, const Task& b) {\n\t\treturn a.priority < b.priority;\n\t});\n}\n---\nInsertion sort is O(n^2) and performs poorly when many tasks have widely varied priorities. std::sort (Introsort) provides O(n log n) and better cache locality, which speeds up the task scheduler's dispatch cycle."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortBinaryMask(std::vector<int>& m) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < m.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < m.size(); j++) {\n\t\t\tif (m[i] > m[j]) std::swap(m[i], m[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortBinaryMask(std::vector<int>& m) {\n\tint count0 = 0;\n\tfor (int x : m) if (x == 0) count0++;\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < m.size(); i++) {\n\t\tm[i] = (i < count0) ? 0 : 1;\n\t}\n}\n---\nFor a simple binary mask, even standard sorting is unnecessary. Counting zeros and re-filling the vector is O(n) with O(1) space, avoiding all swaps and comparisons. This is the fastest possible way to sort binary data."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortColors(std::vector<int>& c) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < c.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < c.size(); j++) {\n\t\t\tif (c[i] > c[j]) std::swap(c[i], c[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortColors(std::vector<int>& c) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = static_cast<int>(c.size()) - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (c[mid] == 0) std::swap(c[low++], c[mid++]);\n\t\telse if (c[mid] == 1) mid++;\n\t\telse std::swap(c[mid], c[high--]);\n\t}\n}\n---\nIf colors are represented as 0, 1, 2 (RGB), selection sort is O(n^2). The Dutch National Flag algorithm sorts them in O(n). This provides massive speedups for low-level image processing filters."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nstruct Ticket {\n\tint level; // 0, 1, 2\n\tint id;\n};\n\nvoid sortTickets(std::vector<Ticket>& t) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < t.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = 0; j < t.size() - 1; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (t[j].level > t[j+1].level) std::swap(t[j], t[j+1]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstruct Ticket {\n\tint level;\n\tint id;\n};\n\nvoid sortTickets(std::vector<Ticket>& t) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = static_cast<int>(t.size()) - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (t[mid].level == 0) std::swap(t[low++], t[mid++]);\n\t\telse if (t[mid].level == 1) mid++;\n\t\telse std::swap(t[mid], t[high--]);\n\t}\n}\n---\nSorting support tickets in O(n^2) delays agent response times. Using O(n) DNF partitioning ensures the help desk dashboard updates instantly, even during peak support hours."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid findSmallest(std::vector<int>& v) {\n\tstd::sort(v.begin(), v.end());\n\tint smallest = v[0];\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid findSmallest(std::vector<int>& v) {\n\tif (v.empty()) return;\n\tauto it = std::min_element(v.begin(), v.end());\n\tint smallest = *it;\n}\n---\nSorting an entire vector O(n log n) to find just the minimum is wasteful. std::min_element finds the value in O(n) time with a single pass, which is significantly faster and uses no extra memory."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortGrades(std::vector<int>& g) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < g.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < g.size(); j++) {\n\t\t\tif (g[i] > g[j]) std::swap(g[i], g[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortGrades(std::vector<int>& g) {\n\tstd::sort(g.begin(), g.end());\n}\n---\nSelection sort is O(n^2). std::sort uses Introsort for O(n log n). For a school district processing thousands of student grades, this reduces report generation time from seconds to milliseconds."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nstruct Log {\n\tint type; // 0: Info, 1: Warning, 2: Error\n\tlong time;\n};\n\nvoid sortLogsByType(std::vector<Log>& l) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < l.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = 0; j < l.size() - 1; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (l[j].type > l[j+1].type) std::swap(l[j], l[j+1]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstruct Log {\n\tint type;\n\tlong time;\n};\n\nvoid sortLogsByType(std::vector<Log>& l) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = static_cast<int>(l.size()) - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (l[mid].type == 0) std::swap(l[low++], l[mid++]);\n\t\telse if (l[mid].type == 1) mid++;\n\t\telse std::swap(l[mid], l[high--]);\n\t}\n}\n---\nBubble sorting logs by type is O(n^2). The Dutch National Flag algorithm provides an O(n) single-pass sort. This is critical for log viewers that need to process megabytes of log data in real-time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortSensorBinary(std::vector<int>& s) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 1; i < s.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tint val = s[i];\n\t\tint j = i - 1;\n\t\twhile (j >= 0 && s[j] > val) {\n\t\t\ts[j+1] = s[j];\n\t\t\tj--;\n\t\t}\n\t\ts[j+1] = val;\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortSensorBinary(std::vector<int>& s) {\n\tint low = 0, high = static_cast<int>(s.size()) - 1;\n\twhile (low < high) {\n\t\tif (s[low] == 0) low++;\n\t\telse if (s[high] == 1) high--;\n\t\telse std::swap(s[low], s[high]);\n\t}\n}\n---\nInsertion sorting sensor flags is O(n^2). A two-pointer partition is O(n). This optimization is vital for embedded sensors with limited battery life, as it minimizes the work done per measurement cycle."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nstruct Order {\n\tint priority; // 0, 1, 2\n\tint val;\n};\n\nvoid sortOrders(std::vector<Order>& o) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < o.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < o.size(); j++) {\n\t\t\tif (o[i].priority > o[j].priority) std::swap(o[i], o[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstruct Order {\n\tint priority;\n\tint val;\n};\n\nvoid sortOrders(std::vector<Order>& o) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = static_cast<int>(o.size()) - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (o[mid].priority == 0) std::swap(o[low++], o[mid++]);\n\t\telse if (o[mid].priority == 1) mid++;\n\t\telse std::swap(o[mid], o[high--]);\n\t}\n}\n---\nSorting delivery orders in O(n^2) limits the scalability of routing software. DNF partitioning in O(n) allows the system to handle thousands of orders per second, improving delivery window accuracy."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortTrafficLights(std::vector<int>& t) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < t.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < t.size(); j++) {\n\t\t\tif (t[i] > t[j]) std::swap(t[i], t[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortTrafficLights(std::vector<int>& t) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = static_cast<int>(t.size()) - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (t[mid] == 0) std::swap(t[low++], t[mid++]);\n\t\telse if (t[mid] == 1) mid++;\n\t\telse std::swap(t[mid], t[high--]);\n\t}\n}\n---\nIn traffic simulation, O(n^2) sorting for light states (Red, Yellow, Green) creates a CPU bottleneck. O(n) partitioning using the Dutch National Flag algorithm allows for much larger simulations with more vehicles and lights."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortInventory(std::vector<int>& inv) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < inv.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = 0; j < inv.size() - 1; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (inv[j] > inv[j+1]) std::swap(inv[j], inv[j+1]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortInventory(std::vector<int>& inv) {\n\tstd::sort(inv.begin(), inv.end());\n}\n---\nBubble sorting a large inventory list is O(n^2). std::sort provides O(n log n). This optimization is essential for stock management systems to handle thousands of SKUs without lagging during search or sort operations."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortBinaryFlags(std::vector<int>& f) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < f.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < f.size(); j++) {\n\t\t\tif (f[i] > f[j]) std::swap(f[i], f[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortBinaryFlags(std::vector<int>& f) {\n\tint low = 0, high = static_cast<int>(f.size()) - 1;\n\twhile (low < high) {\n\t\tif (f[low] == 0) low++;\n\t\telse if (f[high] == 1) high--;\n\t\telse std::swap(f[low], f[high]);\n\t}\n}\n---\nSelection sort for binary flags is O(n^2). A two-pointer approach is O(n). This reduces the time spent in flag reordering, which is highly beneficial for high-throughput data filtering tasks."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nstruct Item {\n\tint weight;\n\tint value;\n};\n\nvoid sortByWeight(std::vector<Item>& items) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < items.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = 0; j < items.size() - 1; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (items[j].weight > items[j+1].weight) std::swap(items[j], items[j+1]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstruct Item {\n\tint weight;\n\tint value;\n};\n\nvoid sortByWeight(std::vector<Item>& items) {\n\tstd::sort(items.begin(), items.end(), [](const Item& a, const Item& b) {\n\t\treturn a.weight < b.weight;\n\t});\n}\n---\nBubble sorting items is O(n^2). std::sort is O(n log n). This optimization is vital for solvers (like the knapsack problem) that need to sort items as a preprocessing step to ensure efficient algorithm execution."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortThreeValArray(std::vector<int>& v) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < v.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < v.size(); j++) {\n\t\t\tif (v[i] > v[j]) std::swap(v[i], v[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortThreeValArray(std::vector<int>& v) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = static_cast<int>(v.size()) - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (v[mid] == 0) std::swap(v[low++], v[mid++]);\n\t\telse if (v[mid] == 1) mid++;\n\t\telse std::swap(v[mid], v[high--]);\n\t}\n}\n---\nFor arrays with only three distinct values, O(n^2) selection sort is very slow. The Dutch National Flag algorithm provides a linear O(n) solution that sorts the array in a single pass with minimal data movement."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nstruct Employee {\n\tint rank; // 0, 1, 2\n\tint id;\n};\n\nvoid sortEmployees(std::vector<Employee>& e) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < e.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = 0; j < e.size() - 1; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (e[j].rank > e[j+1].rank) std::swap(e[j], e[j+1]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstruct Employee {\n\tint rank;\n\tint id;\n};\n\nvoid sortEmployees(std::vector<Employee>& e) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = static_cast<int>(e.size()) - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (e[mid].rank == 0) std::swap(e[low++], e[mid++]);\n\t\telse if (e[mid].rank == 1) mid++;\n\t\telse std::swap(e[mid], e[high--]);\n\t}\n}\n---\nBubble sorting employees by rank is O(n^2). The Dutch National Flag algorithm provides an O(n) single-pass sort. This ensures that organizational charts and payroll lists are processed quickly, even in massive enterprises."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortSignalsByState(std::vector<int>& s) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < s.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < s.size(); j++) {\n\t\t\tif (s[i] > s[j]) std::swap(s[i], s[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortSignalsByState(std::vector<int>& s) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = static_cast<int>(s.size()) - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (s[mid] == 0) std::swap(s[low++], s[mid++]);\n\t\telse if (s[mid] == 1) mid++;\n\t\telse std::swap(s[mid], s[high--]);\n\t}\n}\n---\nSorting signal states in O(n^2) is inefficient for real-time telemetry. Using the O(n) Dutch National Flag algorithm minimizes processing latency, which is essential for low-latency communication systems."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid findTop10(std::vector<int>& v) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < v.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < v.size(); j++) {\n\t\t\tif (v[i] < v[j]) std::swap(v[i], v[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tif (v.size() > 10) v.resize(10);\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid findTop10(std::vector<int>& v) {\n\tif (v.size() > 10) {\n\t\tstd::nth_element(v.begin(), v.begin() + 10, v.end(), std::greater<int>());\n\t\tv.resize(10);\n\t\tstd::sort(v.begin(), v.end(), std::greater<int>());\n\t}\n}\n---\nSorting a whole array O(n^2) just to get 10 elements is a bottleneck. std::nth_element finds the top elements in O(n) time, and sorting just those 10 is negligible. This provides a huge performance gain for large data result sets."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nstruct MemoryPage {\n\tint flag; // 0, 1, 2\n\tint id;\n};\n\nvoid sortPages(std::vector<MemoryPage>& p) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < p.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = 0; j < p.size() - 1; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (p[j].flag > p[j+1].flag) std::swap(p[j], p[j+1]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstruct MemoryPage {\n\tint flag;\n\tint id;\n};\n\nvoid sortPages(std::vector<MemoryPage>& p) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = static_cast<int>(p.size()) - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (p[mid].flag == 0) std::swap(p[low++], p[mid++]);\n\t\telse if (p[mid].flag == 1) mid++;\n\t\telse std::swap(p[mid], p[high--]);\n\t}\n}\n---\nBubble sorting memory pages in O(n^2) increases system overhead during page swaps. The O(n) DNF algorithm ensures page classification is fast, which helps maintain system responsiveness during high memory usage."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortBinaryDataArray(std::vector<int>& d) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 1; i < d.size(); i++) {\n\t\tint val = d[i];\n\t\tint j = i - 1;\n\t\twhile (j >= 0 && d[j] > val) {\n\t\t\td[j+1] = d[j];\n\t\t\tj--;\n\t\t}\n\t\td[j+1] = val;\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortBinaryDataArray(std::vector<int>& d) {\n\tint low = 0, high = static_cast<int>(d.size()) - 1;\n\twhile (low < high) {\n\t\twhile (low < high && d[low] == 0) low++;\n\t\twhile (low < high && d[high] == 1) high--;\n\t\tif (low < high) {\n\t\t\td[low++] = 0;\n\t\t\td[high--] = 1;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nInsertion sorting binary data is O(n^2). A simple two-pointer partition approach sorts the array in O(n). This dramatically reduces the number of comparisons and data moves, which is vital for performance-critical data pipelines."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nstruct Message {\n\tint urgency; // 0, 1, 2\n\tint id;\n};\n\nvoid sortMessages(std::vector<Message>& m) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < m.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < m.size(); j++) {\n\t\t\tif (m[i].urgency > m[j].urgency) std::swap(m[i], m[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstruct Message {\n\tint urgency;\n\tint id;\n};\n\nvoid sortMessages(std::vector<Message>& m) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = static_cast<int>(m.size()) - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (m[mid].urgency == 0) std::swap(m[low++], m[mid++]);\n\t\telse if (m[mid].urgency == 1) mid++;\n\t\telse std::swap(m[mid], m[high--]);\n\t}\n}\n---\nSorting messages by urgency in O(n^2) is inefficient for high-speed messaging apps. Using the O(n) DNF algorithm ensures that urgent messages are prioritized immediately without slowing down the message delivery system."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortReadingFlags(std::vector<int>& r) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < r.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = 0; j < r.size() - 1; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (r[j] > r[j+1]) std::swap(r[j], r[j+1]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortReadingFlags(std::vector<int>& r) {\n\tstd::sort(r.begin(), r.end());\n}\n---\nBubble sorting reading flags in O(n^2) is inefficient. std::sort provides O(n log n). This optimization is critical for reading processing systems to handle large datasets of flags without lagging during data analysis."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortThreeStateFlags(std::vector<int>& f) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < f.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < f.size(); j++) {\n\t\t\tif (f[i] > f[j]) std::swap(f[i], f[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortThreeStateFlags(std::vector<int>& f) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = static_cast<int>(f.size()) - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (f[mid] == 0) std::swap(f[low++], f[mid++]);\n\t\telse if (f[mid] == 1) mid++;\n\t\telse std::swap(f[mid], f[high--]);\n\t}\n}\n---\nSorting flags with only three states in O(n^2) is very slow. The Dutch National Flag algorithm provides an O(n) single-pass sort. This ensures that state classification is fast and efficient, which is vital for high-performance applications."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nstruct Record {\n\tint category; // 0, 1, 2\n\tint id;\n};\n\nvoid sortRecords(std::vector<Record>& r) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < r.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = 0; j < r.size() - 1; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (r[j].category > r[j+1].category) std::swap(r[j], r[j+1]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstruct Record {\n\tint category;\n\tint id;\n};\n\nvoid sortRecords(std::vector<Record>& r) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = static_cast<int>(r.size()) - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (r[mid].category == 0) std::swap(r[low++], r[mid++]);\n\t\telse if (r[mid].category == 1) mid++;\n\t\telse std::swap(r[mid], r[high--]);\n\t}\n}\n---\nBubble sorting records by category is O(n^2). The Dutch National Flag algorithm provides an O(n) single-pass sort. This ensures that record categorization is fast, which is critical for large-scale data processing systems."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid findTop5Scores(std::vector<int>& s) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < s.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < s.size(); j++) {\n\t\t\tif (s[i] < s[j]) std::swap(s[i], s[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tif (s.size() > 5) s.resize(5);\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid findTop5Scores(std::vector<int>& s) {\n\tif (s.size() > 5) {\n\t\tstd::nth_element(s.begin(), s.begin() + 5, s.end(), std::greater<int>());\n\t\ts.resize(5);\n\t\tstd::sort(s.begin(), s.end(), std::greater<int>());\n\t}\n}\n---\nSorting a whole scores array O(n^2) just to get 5 elements is a bottleneck. std::nth_element finds the top elements in O(n) time, and sorting just those 5 is negligible. This provides a huge performance gain for large score datasets."}
