{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nstruct DataLog {\n\tint type; // 0, 1, 2\n\tint id;\n};\n\nvoid sortDataLogs(std::vector<DataLog>& l) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < l.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < l.size(); j++) {\n\t\t\tif (l[i].type > l[j].type) std::swap(l[i], l[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstruct DataLog {\n\tint type;\n\tint id;\n};\n\nvoid sortDataLogs(std::vector<DataLog>& l) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = static_cast<int>(l.size()) - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (l[mid].type == 0) std::swap(l[low++], l[mid++]);\n\t\telse if (l[mid].type == 1) mid++;\n\t\telse std::swap(l[mid], l[high--]);\n\t}\n}\n---\nSorting data logs by type in O(n^2) is inefficient. The O(n) DNF algorithm provides a single-pass sort. This ensures that log classification is fast, which is critical for real-time data monitoring systems."}
