{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nstruct LogEntryRecord {\n\tint type; // 0, 1, 2\n\tint id;\n};\n\nvoid sortLogEntryRecords(std::vector<LogEntryRecord>& l) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < l.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < l.size(); j++) {\n\t\t\tif (l[i].type > l[j].type) std::swap(l[i], l[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstruct LogEntryRecord {\n\tint type;\n\tint id;\n};\n\nvoid sortLogEntryRecords(std::vector<LogEntryRecord>& l) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = static_cast<int>(l.size()) - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (l[mid].type == 0) std::swap(l[low++], l[mid++]);\n\t\telse if (l[mid].type == 1) mid++;\n\t\telse std::swap(l[mid], l[high--]);\n\t}\n}\n---\nSorting log entries by type in O(n^2) is inefficient. The O(n) DNF algorithm provides a single-pass sort. This ensures that log classification is fast, which is critical for real-time data monitoring systems."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortReadingEntryArray(std::vector<int>& r) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < r.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < r.size(); j++) {\n\t\t\tif (r[i] > r[j]) std::swap(r[i], r[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortReadingEntryArray(std::vector<int>& r) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = static_cast<int>(r.size()) - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (r[mid] == 0) std::swap(r[low++], r[mid++]);\n\t\telse if (r[mid] == 1) mid++;\n\t\telse std::swap(r[mid], r[high--]);\n\t}\n}\n---\nSorting reading entries in O(n^2) is inefficient. The O(n) DNF algorithm provides a single-pass sort. This ensures that entry classification is fast, which is critical for high-performance reading processing systems."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nint sumMatrix(const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& matrix) {\n\tint total = 0;\n\tsize_t rows = matrix.size();\n\tsize_t cols = matrix[0].size();\n\tfor (size_t j = 0; j < cols; ++j) {\n\t\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < rows; ++i) {\n\t\t\ttotal += matrix[i][j];\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn total;\n}", "output": "int sumMatrix(const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& matrix) {\n\tint total = 0;\n\tfor (const auto& row : matrix) {\n\t\tfor (int val : row) {\n\t\t\ttotal += val;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn total;\n}\n---\nColumn-major traversal of a row-major matrix is a cache-miss nightmare. By switching to row-major traversal (inner loop over columns), you take advantage of spatial locality. Your CPU can prefetch the contiguous memory, turning a stuttering crawl into a linear sprint."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n\nvoid processStrings(std::vector[std::string](https://www.google.com/search?q=std::string) list) {\n\tfor (auto s : list) {\n\t\t// processing\n\t}\n}", "output": "void processStrings(const std::vector[std::string](https://www.google.com/search?q=std::string)& list) {\n\tfor (const auto& s : list) {\n\t\t// processing\n\t}\n}\n---\nPassing the vector and the strings inside it by value triggers deep copies of every single element. By using `const` references, you avoid unnecessary heap allocations and deallocations. It's essentially the difference between photocopying a book 100 times and just pointing at the original."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid removeZeros(std::vector<int>& v) {\n\tfor (auto it = v.begin(); it != v.end(); ) {\n\t\tif (*it == 0) {\n\t\t\tit = v.erase(it);\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\t++it;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid removeZeros(std::vector<int>& v) {\n\tv.erase(std::remove(v.begin(), v.end(), 0), v.end());\n}\n---\nCalling `erase()` inside a loop on a `std::vector` is O(n²) because every erasure shifts all subsequent elements. The Erase-Remove idiom is O(n); it shifts elements in a single pass and performs one final resize. This is a classic case of 'work smarter, not harder.'"}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <string>\n\nbool isPrefix(https://www.google.com/search?q=std::string s, https://www.google.com/search?q=std::string prefix) {\n\treturn s.substr(0, prefix.size()) == prefix;\n}", "output": "#include <string_view>\n\nbool isPrefix(https://www.google.com/search?q=std::string_view s, https://www.google.com/search?q=std::string_view prefix) {\n\tif (s.size() < prefix.size()) return false;\n\treturn s.substr(0, prefix.size()) == prefix;\n}\n---\n`std::string::substr` creates a brand new string, which involves a heap allocation. `std::string_view` (C++17) just points to the existing memory. You're getting the same logic with zero extra allocations. Your memory manager will thank you."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nstruct Data {\n\tint x, y, z;\n};\n\nvoid addData(std::vector<Data>& v, int x, int y, int z) {\n\tData d = {x, y, z};\n\tv.push_back(d);\n}", "output": "void addData(std::vector<Data>& v, int x, int y, int z) {\n\tv.emplace_back(x, y, z);\n}\n---\n`push_back` often creates a temporary object and then moves/copies it into the vector. `emplace_back` constructs the object directly in the vector's memory. It’s the difference between buying furniture and building it inside the house versus buying it and trying to squeeze it through the front door."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <list>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortData(std::list<int>& l) {\n\tstd::vector<int> v(l.begin(), l.end());\n\tstd::sort(v.begin(), v.end());\n\tl.assign(v.begin(), v.end());\n}", "output": "void sortData(std::list<int>& l) {\n\tl.sort();\n}\n---\nWhile transferring a `list` to a `vector` to sort is sometimes faster due to cache locality, for a `std::list`, you should generally use its member function `l.sort()`. More importantly, if you need to sort frequently, `std::list` is likely the wrong container—use `std::vector` from the start to avoid pointer-chasing overhead."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <iostream>\n\nvoid printLargeData(int n) {\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tstd::cout << i << std::endl;\n\t}\n}", "output": "void printLargeData(int n) {\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tstd::cout << i << '\n';\n\t}\n\tstd::cout.flush();\n}\n---\n`std::endl` doesn't just print a newline; it forces a buffer flush. Flushing the output buffer  times is incredibly slow. Use `'\\n'` and let the stream handle flushing naturally, or flush once at the end. Your I/O throughput will skyrocket."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <cmath>\n\nvoid compute(std::vector<float>& data) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < data.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tdata[i] = data[i] * std::sin(3.14159 / 2.0);\n\t}\n}", "output": "void compute(std::vector<float>& data) {\n\tconst float factor = std::sin(3.14159 / 2.0);\n\tfor (float& val : data) {\n\t\tval *= factor;\n\t}\n}\n---\nThis is 'Loop Invariant Code Motion.' The `std::sin` calculation yields the same result every time. Moving it outside the loop prevents thousands of redundant function calls. Even if the compiler optimizes this, explicit movement ensures performance across all build configurations."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nbool allPositive(const std::vector<int>& v) {\n\tbool positive = true;\n\tfor (int i : v) {\n\t\tif (i <= 0) positive = false;\n\t}\n\treturn positive;\n}", "output": "bool allPositive(const std::vector<int>& v) {\n\tfor (int i : v) {\n\t\tif (i <= 0) return false;\n\t}\n\treturn true;\n}\n---\nEarly exit! The original code continues checking elements even after finding a non-positive one. Returning immediately on failure can save thousands of iterations in large datasets. It's the computational equivalent of stopping at the first closed door instead of trying every door in the hallway."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <unordered_map>\n\nvoid insertMany(std::unordered_map<int, int>& m, int n) {\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tm[i] = i * 2;\n\t}\n}", "output": "void insertMany(std::unordered_map<int, int>& m, int n) {\n\tm.reserve(n);\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tm[i] = i * 2;\n\t}\n}\n---\nHash maps trigger expensive 'rehash' operations as they grow. By using `reserve()`, you allocate the necessary buckets upfront. This prevents multiple reallocations and keeps your insertion complexity closer to a true O(1)."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <numeric>\n\nlong long sumVector(std::vector<int> v) {\n\treturn std::accumulate(v.begin(), v.end(), 0LL);\n}", "output": "long long sumVector(const std::vector<int>& v) {\n\treturn std::accumulate(v.begin(), v.end(), 0LL);\n}\n---\nPass by reference! The original code copies the entire vector onto the stack. For a vector with a million integers, that's 4MB of wasted copying per function call. `const reference` gives you the data without the baggage."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <memory>\n\nvoid createObjects() {\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < 1000; ++i) {\n\t\tauto p = std::make_unique<int>(i);\n\t}\n}", "output": "void createObjects() {\n\tstd::vector<int> v;\n\tv.reserve(1000);\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < 1000; ++i) {\n\t\tv.push_back(i);\n\t}\n}\n---\nHeap allocations (`new`, `make_unique`) are slow compared to stack or contiguous vector allocations. If you don't need individual heap pointers, use a vector to keep data together. It's better for the allocator and significantly better for your CPU cache."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "struct Point { double x, y; };\n\nbool compare(Point a, Point b) {\n\treturn a.x < b.x;\n}", "output": "struct Point { double x, y; };\n\nbool compare(const Point& a, const Point& b) {\n\treturn a.x < b.x;\n}\n---\nEven for small structs, passing by reference is a good habit. While a 16-byte struct might fit in registers, consistently passing by `const reference` prevents regressions if the struct grows (e.g., adding a `std::string` name member) in the future."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid clearAndFill(std::vector<int>& v, int n) {\n\tv.clear();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) v.push_back(i);\n}", "output": "void clearAndFill(std::vector<int>& v, int n) {\n\tv.assign(n, 0); // or v.resize(n) if values are set later\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) v[i] = i;\n}\n---\n`clear()` doesn't deallocate memory, but `push_back()` still has to check for capacity. If you know the size, `resize()` or `assign()` is more direct. Better yet, if the vector is reused, the capacity is already there—avoiding `push_back`'s conditional checks inside the loop."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <set>\n\nbool exists(const std::set<int>& s, int val) {\n\tfor (auto x : s) {\n\t\tif (x == val) return true;\n\t}\n\treturn false;\n}", "output": "bool exists(const std::set<int>& s, int val) {\n\treturn s.find(val) != s.end();\n}\n---\nSearching a `std::set` using a loop is O(n). Using `s.find()` is O(log n) because it uses the underlying red-black tree. You are turning a manual crawl through every node into a targeted search."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <string>\n\nvoid filter(std::vector[std::string](https://www.google.com/search?q=std::string)& v) {\n\tstd::vector[std::string](https://www.google.com/search?q=std::string) temp;\n\tfor (const auto& s : v) {\n\t\tif (s.size() > 5) temp.push_back(s);\n\t}\n\tv = temp;\n}", "output": "void filter(std::vector[std::string](https://www.google.com/search?q=std::string)& v) {\n\tstd::vector[std::string](https://www.google.com/search?q=std::string) temp;\n\ttemp.reserve(v.size());\n\tfor (auto& s : v) {\n\t\tif (s.size() > 5) temp.push_back(std::move(s));\n\t}\n\tv = std::move(temp);\n}\n---\nMoving strings is significantly faster than copying them. In the original, every filtered string is copied into `temp`, then copied again during `v = temp`. Using `std::move` transfers ownership of the heap-allocated string buffer, avoiding new allocations entirely."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid process(const std::vector<int>& v) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < v.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tint val = v.at(i);\n\t\t// use val\n\t}\n}", "output": "void process(const std::vector<int>& v) {\n\tfor (int val : v) {\n\t\t// use val\n\t}\n}\n---\n`v.at(i)` performs bounds checking on every single access, throwing an exception if out of range. In a tight loop, this overhead adds up. Range-based for loops or `v[i]` skip the check, which is safe if you know your indices are valid."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <map>\n\nvoid update(std::map<int, int>& m, int key) {\n\tm[key] = m[key] + 1;\n}", "output": "void update(std::map<int, int>& m, int key) {\n\t++m[key];\n}\n---\nActually, `m[key] = m[key] + 1` performs two lookups: one to read and one to write. `++m[key]` is technically cleaner, but even better: `m.try_emplace(key, 0).first->second++;` for complex objects. For simple ints, the compiler often fixes this, but direct mutation is always safer."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <string>\n\nvoid append(https://www.google.com/search?q=std::string& s, char c) {\n\ts = s + c;\n}", "output": "void append(https://www.google.com/search?q=std::string& s, char c) {\n\ts += c;\n}\n---\n`s = s + c` creates a temporary string object to hold the result of the addition, then copies it back to `s`. `s += c` (or `s.push_back(c)`) modifies the existing buffer in-place, which is much more efficient."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "bool isTrue(int x) {\n\tif (x == 1) return true;\n\telse return false;\n}", "output": "bool isTrue(int x) {\n\treturn x == 1;\n}\n---\nThis isn't just about brevity; it removes branching logic. Modern CPUs use branch prediction, and while this is simple, returning the result of the comparison directly is cleaner for the pipeline and the programmer's eyes."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortDescending(std::vector<int>& v) {\n\tstd::sort(v.begin(), v.end());\n\tstd::reverse(v.begin(), v.end());\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n#include <functional>\n\nvoid sortDescending(std::vector<int>& v) {\n\tstd::sort(v.begin(), v.end(), std::greater<int>());\n}\n---\nDon't double back. Sorting then reversing is two passes over the memory. Sorting with `std::greater` does it in one pass. It's more efficient and expresses intent more clearly."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <cmath>\n\nfloat inverseSqrt(float x) {\n\treturn 1.0f / std::sqrt(x);\n}", "output": "float inverseSqrt(float x) {\n\treturn 1.0f / std::sqrt(x);\n}\n---\n*Wait, a twist!* In modern C++, the 'Fast Inverse Square Root' (Quake III style) is usually slower than `1.0 / std::sqrt` because CPUs now have dedicated hardware instructions (`RSQRTSS`) for this. Stick to the standard library unless you're on very specialized hardware."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid removeLast(std::vector<int>& v) {\n\tv.erase(v.end() - 1);\n}", "output": "void removeLast(std::vector<int>& v) {\n\tif (!v.empty()) v.pop_back();\n}\n---\n`pop_back()` is the idiomatic and highly optimized way to remove the last element. While `erase(v.end() - 1)` is O(1), `pop_back()` is more readable and explicitly avoids the complexity of the general-purpose `erase` function."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid check(std::vector<int> v) {\n\tif (v.size() > 0) { /*...*/ }\n}", "output": "void check(const std::vector<int>& v) {\n\tif (!v.empty()) { /*...*/ }\n}\n---\nUse `empty()` instead of `size() > 0`. For some containers (like `std::list` in older C++ standards), `size()` could be O(n), whereas `empty()` is always O(1). Plus, it's more expressive."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nint findFirst(const std::vector<int>& v, int target) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < v.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tif (v[i] == target) return i;\n\t}\n\treturn -1;\n}", "output": "#include <algorithm>\n\nint findFirst(const std::vector<int>& v, int target) {\n\tauto it = std::find(v.begin(), v.end(), target);\n\tif (it != v.end()) return std::distance(v.begin(), it);\n\treturn -1;\n}\n---\n`std::find` is highly optimized (often using SIMD instructions under the hood). While a manual loop is O(n), the standard library version is likely faster on a per-byte basis due to platform-specific optimizations."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid add(std::vector<int>& v, int val) {\n\tv.insert(v.begin(), val);\n}", "output": "#include <deque>\n\nvoid add(std::deque<int>& d, int val) {\n\td.push_front(val);\n}\n---\nInserting at the front of a `std::vector` is O(n) because every single element must be moved. If you need frequent front-insertions, `std::deque` is O(1) for both ends. Choose the right tool for the job."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid uniqueSort(std::vector<int>& v) {\n\tstd::sort(v.begin(), v.end());\n\tstd::vector<int> v2;\n\tfor (int x : v) {\n\t\tif (v2.empty() || x != v2.back()) v2.push_back(x);\n\t}\n\tv = v2;\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid uniqueSort(std::vector<int>& v) {\n\tstd::sort(v.begin(), v.end());\n\tv.erase(std::unique(v.begin(), v.end()), v.end());\n}\n---\n`std::unique` is an in-place algorithm that moves duplicates to the end in O(n). This avoids creating a second vector and performing all those extra copies/allocations. In-place is almost always the winner for memory-bound tasks."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid process(std::vector<int>& v) {\n\tfor (auto it = v.begin(); it != v.end(); ++it) {\n\t\t// ...\n\t}\n}", "output": "void process(std::vector<int>& v) {\n\tfor (int& x : v) {\n\t\t// ...\n\t}\n}\n---\nRange-based for loops are not just 'syntactic sugar'; they prevent common iterator errors and allow the compiler to optimize the loop more easily. They also clearly show whether you are modifying elements (`int&`) or just reading them (`const int&`)."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <string>\n\nvoid log(https://www.google.com/search?q=std::string msg) {\n\t// log it\n}", "output": "#include <string_view>\n\nvoid log(https://www.google.com/search?q=std::string_view msg) {\n\t// log it\n}\n---\nPassing `std::string` by value creates a copy. Passing by `const std::string&` is better but forces a `std::string` object to exist. `std::string_view` accepts string literals, `std::string`, and char arrays without any allocation. It’s the ultimate 'read-only' string type."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <numeric>\n\nvoid initialize(std::vector<int>& v) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < v.size(); ++i) v[i] = 0;\n}", "output": "#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid initialize(std::vector<int>& v) {\n\tstd::fill(v.begin(), v.end(), 0);\n}\n---\n`std::fill` is often optimized to use `memset` for primitive types like `int`. `memset` is an assembly-level block write that is significantly faster than a manual loop assigning one integer at a time."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "struct BigStruct { int arr[1000]; };\n\nBigStruct create() {\n\tBigStruct b;\n\treturn b;\n}", "output": "BigStruct create() {\n\treturn BigStruct{};\n}\n---\nModern C++ uses Return Value Optimization (RVO). By returning a temporary directly, the compiler can construct the object directly in the caller's memory space, skipping the copy/move entirely. Your 4KB struct just became 'free' to return."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid doubleAll(std::vector<int>& v) {\n\tsize_t n = v.size();\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tv.push_back(v[i] * 2);\n\t}\n}", "output": "void doubleAll(std::vector<int>& v) {\n\tsize_t n = v.size();\n\tv.reserve(n * 2);\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n\t\tv.push_back(v[i] * 2);\n\t}\n}\n---\nCalling `push_back` enough times to trigger a resize during a loop is costly. Reserving the space upfront ensures a single allocation and guarantees that your iterators/indices remain stable throughout the process."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <map>\n\nint getCount(std::map<int, int>& m, int key) {\n\treturn m[key];\n}", "output": "int getCount(const std::map<int, int>& m, int key) {\n\tauto it = m.find(key);\n\treturn (it != m.end()) ? it->second : 0;\n}\n---\nUsing `operator[]` on a `std::map` is not a 'read' operation; it’s a 'read-or-insert' operation. If the key doesn't exist, it inserts a default-constructed value. This bloats your map and prevents the function from being `const`."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortPtrs(std::vector<int*> v) {\n\tstd::sort(v.begin(), v.end(), [](int* a, int* b) {\n\t\treturn *a < *b;\n\t});\n}", "output": "/* Use a vector of objects if possible */\n---\nSorting a vector of pointers is slow because every comparison is a cache miss (pointer chasing). If you can, store objects by value in a `std::vector`. If you must use pointers, your CPU will spend more time waiting for RAM than actually comparing numbers."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "int factorial(int n) {\n\tif (n <= 1) return 1;\n\treturn n * factorial(n - 1);\n}", "output": "constexpr int factorial(int n) {\n\tint res = 1;\n\tfor (int i = 2; i <= n; ++i) res *= i;\n\treturn res;\n}\n---\nRecursion has stack overhead. An iterative approach is faster and avoids stack overflow. Adding `constexpr` allows the compiler to calculate the value at compile-time if the input is known, reducing runtime cost to zero."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid filterPositive(std::vector<int>& v) {\n\tstd::vector<int> out;\n\tfor (int x : v) if (x > 0) out.push_back(x);\n\tv = out;\n}", "output": "#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid filterPositive(std::vector<int>& v) {\n\tv.erase(std::remove_if(v.begin(), v.end(), [](int x) { return x <= 0; }), v.end());\n}\n---\nThe 'Remove-If' idiom is O(n) and in-place. Creating a new vector involves heap allocation and copying. Staying in-place is the golden rule for high-performance C++ collection manipulation."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nstruct Node { int val; Node* next; };\n\nvoid traverse(Node* head) {\n\twhile(head) {\n\t\thead = head->next;\n\t}\n}", "output": "/* Use std::vector<int> instead */\n---\nLinked lists (`Node*`) are the enemy of modern CPU caches. Each `next` pointer points to a random location in memory. Switching to a `std::vector` puts your data in a contiguous block, allowing the CPU to prefetch data and run at full speed."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid process(std::vector<bool>& v) {\n\tfor (auto b : v) { /*...*/ }\n}", "output": "/* Use std::vector<uint8_t> or std::deque<bool> if speed matters */\n---\n`std::vector<bool>` is a space-optimized specialization that packs bits. However, this packing makes access slower because of the bitmasking required. If you need raw speed and don't care about memory, `std::vector<char>` is often faster."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <unordered_map>\n\nint lookup(std::unordered_map<int, int>& m, int k) {\n\treturn m[k];\n}", "output": "/* Use a sorted std::vector with std::lower_bound for small datasets */\n---\nHash maps have overhead. For small sets (e.g., < 100 elements), a sorted `std::vector` with binary search is often faster because it’s cache-friendly and avoids the cost of hashing the key."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "float fastSquare(float x) {\n\treturn pow(x, 2);\n}", "output": "float fastSquare(float x) {\n\treturn x * x;\n}\n---\n`std::pow` is a general-purpose power function designed to handle fractional exponents. It is significantly slower than simple multiplication. Use `x * x` for squares—it’s a single CPU cycle."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid addTen(std::vector<int>& v) {\n\tfor (auto it = v.begin(); it != v.end(); ++it) {\n\t\t*it += 10;\n\t}\n}", "output": "void addTen(std::vector<int>& v) {\n\tfor (int& x : v) x += 10;\n}\n---\nWhile the performance is the same, range-based for loops are more readable and less error-prone. In some cases, they also help the compiler prove that the end iterator doesn't change, enabling better loop unrolling."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <atomic>\n\nint counter = 0;\nvoid inc() { counter++; }", "output": "std::atomic<int> counter{0};\nvoid inc() { counter.fetch_add(1, std::memory_order_relaxed); }\n---\nIf you're in a multi-threaded environment, `counter++` is a race condition. Using a mutex is slow. `std::atomic` with `memory_order_relaxed` provides thread-safety with near-zero overhead on most modern CPUs."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid shrink(std::vector<int>& v) {\n\tv.resize(10);\n}", "output": "void shrink(std::vector<int>& v) {\n\tv.resize(10);\n\tv.shrink_to_fit();\n}\n---\n`resize()` changes the size but not the capacity. If you significantly reduce the size of a vector and want to free up that memory for the rest of the system, `shrink_to_fit()` is necessary. Use it sparingly, as it triggers a new allocation."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "void swap(int& a, int& b) {\n\tint temp = a;\n\ta = b;\n\tb = temp;\n}", "output": "#include <utility>\n\nvoid swap(int& a, int& b) {\n\tstd::swap(a, b);\n}\n---\nAlways use `std::swap`. For primitives, it's the same, but for complex types (like `std::vector`), `std::swap` is specialized to just swap internal pointers (O(1)) rather than copying the entire content (O(n))."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "double getDist(double x, double y) {\n\treturn sqrt(x*x + y*y);\n}", "output": "double getDistSq(double x, double y) {\n\treturn x*x + y*y;\n}\n---\nIf you are just comparing distances (e.g., `distA < distB`), compare the squared distances instead. `sqrt` is expensive; avoid it whenever possible by staying in 'squared space.'"}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid work() {\n\tstd::vector<int> v(1000, 0);\n\t// do work\n}", "output": "void work() {\n\tstatic std::vector<int> v(1000, 0);\n\t// reset and do work\n}\n---\nIf a function is called thousands of times, allocating and deallocating a vector on every call is wasteful. Using a `static` vector (or a pool) allows you to reuse the same capacity over and over. Just remember to reset the values if needed!"}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid bubbleSort(std::vector<int>& arr) {\n\tint n = arr.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < n - i - 1; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (arr[j] > arr[j + 1]) {\n\t\t\t\tstd::swap(arr[j], arr[j + 1]);\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid bubbleSort(std::vector<int>& arr) {\n\tstd::sort(arr.begin(), arr.end());\n}\n---\nThe optimization replaces a nested-loop bubble sort with std::sort, which typically uses Introsort. This improves the time complexity from O(n^2) to O(n log n), significantly reducing execution time for large datasets. It also benefits from better cache locality and hardware-level optimizations."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortColors(std::vector<int>& nums) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tfor (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (nums[i] > nums[j]) {\n\t\t\t\tint temp = nums[i];\n\t\t\t\tnums[i] = nums[j];\n\t\t\t\tnums[j] = temp;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortColors(std::vector<int>& nums) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = nums.size() - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (nums[mid] == 0) {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(nums[low], nums[mid]);\n\t\t\tlow++;\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else if (nums[mid] == 1) {\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(nums[mid], nums[high]);\n\t\t\thigh--;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nThe code is optimized using the Dutch National Flag algorithm, which reduces the complexity from O(n^2) to O(n). This single-pass approach is highly efficient for sorting arrays with a small, fixed range of values (0, 1, 2) by using three pointers to partition the elements in place."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <string>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstruct Product {\n\tint id;\n\tdouble price;\n\tstd::string name;\n};\n\nvoid sortProducts(std::vector<Product>& products) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < products.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = 0; j < products.size() - 1; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (products[j].price > products[j+1].price) {\n\t\t\t\tstd::swap(products[j], products[j+1]);\n\t\t\t} else if (products[j].price == products[j+1].price && products[j].name > products[j+1].name) {\n\t\t\t\tstd::swap(products[j], products[j+1]);\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <string>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstruct Product {\n\tint id;\n\tdouble price;\n\tstd::string name;\n};\n\nvoid sortProducts(std::vector<Product>& products) {\n\tstd::sort(products.begin(), products.end(), [](const Product& a, const Product& b) {\n\t\tif (a.price != b.price) return a.price < b.price;\n\t\treturn a.name < b.name;\n\t});\n}\n---\nThe manual bubble sort implementation is replaced with std::sort using a custom lambda comparator. This changes the complexity from O(n^2) to O(n log n) and provides a more idiomatic, maintainable way to handle multi-field sorting while maximizing comparison efficiency."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid findTopThree(std::vector<int>& scores) {\n\tint n = scores.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tfor (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (scores[i] < scores[j]) {\n\t\t\t\tstd::swap(scores[i], scores[j]);\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid findTopThree(std::vector<int>& scores) {\n\tif (scores.size() > 3) {\n\t\tstd::partial_sort(scores.begin(), scores.begin() + 3, scores.end(), std::greater<int>());\n\t}\n}\n---\nThe O(n^2) full sort is replaced with std::partial_sort, which has a complexity of O(n log k), where k is the number of elements to sort (3 in this case). This avoids the overhead of sorting the entire container when only the top elements are required, resulting in significant performance gains for large input vectors."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortSmallRange(std::vector<int>& data) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 1; i < data.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tint key = data[i];\n\t\tint j = i - 1;\n\t\twhile (j >= 0 && data[j] > key) {\n\t\t\tdata[j + 1] = data[j];\n\t\t\tj = j - 1;\n\t\t}\n\t\tdata[j + 1] = key;\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortSmallRange(std::vector<int>& data) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = static_cast<int>(data.size()) - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (data[mid] == 0) {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(data[low], data[mid]);\n\t\t\tlow++;\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else if (data[mid] == 1) {\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(data[mid], data[high]);\n\t\t\thigh--;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nThe insertion sort (O(n^2)) is replaced with the Dutch National Flag algorithm (O(n)) for data containing only values 0, 1, and 2. This linear-time solution eliminates unnecessary comparisons and shifts, providing the most efficient possible sort for this specific value range."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid getMedian(std::vector<int>& values) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < values.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = 0; j < values.size() - 1; ++j) {\n\t\t\tif (values[j] > values[j+1]) {\n\t\t\t\tstd::swap(values[j], values[j+1]);\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid getMedian(std::vector<int>& values) {\n\tif (values.empty()) return;\n\tstd::nth_element(values.begin(), values.begin() + values.size() / 2, values.end());\n}\n---\nInstead of an O(n^2) bubble sort, this uses std::nth_element to find the median in O(n) average time. This avoids sorting the entire array when only the element at a specific position is needed, drastically improving performance for large datasets."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <string>\n\nstruct User {\n\tstd::string username;\n\tint rank;\n};\n\nvoid sortUsersByRank(std::vector<User>& users) {\n\tint n = users.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < n - i - 1; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (users[j].rank > users[j + 1].rank) {\n\t\t\t\tUser temp = users[j];\n\t\t\t\tusers[j] = users[j + 1];\n\t\t\t\tusers[j + 1] = temp;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <string>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstruct User {\n\tstd::string username;\n\tint rank;\n};\n\nvoid sortUsersByRank(std::vector<User>& users) {\n\tstd::sort(users.begin(), users.end(), [](const User& a, const User& b) {\n\t\treturn a.rank < b.rank;\n\t});\n}\n---\nThe O(n^2) bubble sort is replaced by std::sort, which utilizes Introsort for O(n log n) average and worst-case performance. This optimization is particularly effective for objects with string members, as it minimizes the number of expensive move/copy operations required during the sorting process."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid selectionSort(std::vector<int>& arr) {\n\tint n = arr.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {\n\t\tint min_idx = i;\n\t\tfor (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (arr[j] < arr[min_idx])\n\t\t\t\tmin_idx = j;\n\t\t}\n\t\tint temp = arr[min_idx];\n\t\tarr[min_idx] = arr[i];\n\t\tarr[i] = temp;\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid selectionSort(std::vector<int>& arr) {\n\tstd::sort(arr.begin(), arr.end());\n}\n---\nSelection sort's O(n^2) complexity is inefficient for general sorting tasks. Replacing it with std::sort provides O(n log n) performance using a highly optimized implementation that leverages cache-friendly access patterns and highly efficient partitioning logic."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortThreeValues(std::vector<int>& vec) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < vec.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < vec.size(); ++j) {\n\t\t\tif (vec[i] > vec[j]) {\n\t\t\t\tint t = vec[i];\n\t\t\t\tvec[i] = vec[j];\n\t\t\t\tvec[j] = t;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortThreeValues(std::vector<int>& vec) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = static_cast<int>(vec.size()) - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (vec[mid] == 0) {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(vec[low], vec[mid]);\n\t\t\tlow++;\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else if (vec[mid] == 1) {\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(vec[mid], vec[high]);\n\t\t\thigh--;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nThe O(n^2) nested loop is optimized using the Dutch National Flag algorithm to achieve O(n) time complexity. This is the optimal approach for containers holding only three distinct values, as it performs the sort in a single pass with minimal swaps."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortNearlySorted(std::vector<int>& arr) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < arr.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = 0; j < arr.size() - 1; ++j) {\n\t\t\tif (arr[j] > arr[j+1]) {\n\t\t\t\tstd::swap(arr[j], arr[j+1]);\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortNearlySorted(std::vector<int>& arr) {\n\tstd::sort(arr.begin(), arr.end());\n}\n---\nWhile bubble sort is O(n^2), std::sort is O(n log n). For nearly sorted data, modern implementations of std::sort (like those using Timsort-inspired logic or adaptive Introsort) are extremely fast, significantly outperforming a naive bubble sort even when the data requires few swaps."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortBinaryArray(std::vector<int>& nums) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < n - i - 1; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (nums[j] > nums[j + 1]) {\n\t\t\t\tint temp = nums[j];\n\t\t\t\tnums[j] = nums[j + 1];\n\t\t\t\tnums[j + 1] = temp;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortBinaryArray(std::vector<int>& nums) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = nums.size() - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (nums[mid] == 0) {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(nums[low], nums[mid]);\n\t\t\tlow++;\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else if (nums[mid] == 1) {\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(nums[mid], nums[high]);\n\t\t\thigh--;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nReplacing the O(n^2) bubble sort with a single-pass Dutch National Flag algorithm reduces time complexity to O(n). Although the original was binary, the 3-way partition logic is robust and ensures linear time performance for containers with few unique keys."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <string>\n\nstruct Task {\n\tint priority;\n\tint duration;\n};\n\nvoid sortTasks(std::vector<Task>& tasks) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < tasks.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < tasks.size(); j++) {\n\t\t\tif (tasks[i].priority < tasks[j].priority) {\n\t\t\t\tstd::swap(tasks[i], tasks[j]);\n\t\t\t} else if (tasks[i].priority == tasks[j].priority && tasks[i].duration > tasks[j].duration) {\n\t\t\t\tstd::swap(tasks[i], tasks[j]);\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstruct Task {\n\tint priority;\n\tint duration;\n};\n\nvoid sortTasks(std::vector<Task>& tasks) {\n\tstd::sort(tasks.begin(), tasks.end(), [](const Task& a, const Task& b) {\n\t\tif (a.priority != b.priority) return a.priority > b.priority;\n\t\treturn a.duration < b.duration;\n\t});\n}\n---\nThe O(n^2) selection-style sort is optimized to O(n log n) using std::sort with a lambda. This approach is much more efficient for multi-criteria sorting and leverages modern C++'s optimized sorting primitives which handle swaps and comparisons more effectively."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid findKthSmallest(std::vector<int>& vec, int k) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < vec.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = 0; j < vec.size() - 1; ++j) {\n\t\t\tif (vec[j] > vec[j+1]) {\n\t\t\t\tstd::swap(vec[j], vec[j+1]);\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid findKthSmallest(std::vector<int>& vec, int k) {\n\tif (k >= 0 && k < static_cast<int>(vec.size())) {\n\t\tstd::nth_element(vec.begin(), vec.begin() + k, vec.end());\n\t}\n}\n---\nThe original bubble sort has O(n^2) complexity. Using std::nth_element reduces this to O(n) average time complexity, as it only partitions the array enough to place the k-th element in its correct sorted position, skipping the work of a full sort."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortTrafficLights(std::vector<int>& lights) {\n\tint n = lights.size();\n\tfor (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tint key = lights[i];\n\t\tint j = i - 1;\n\t\twhile (j >= 0 && lights[j] > key) {\n\t\t\tlights[j + 1] = lights[j];\n\t\t\tj--;\n\t\t}\n\t\tlights[j + 1] = key;\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortTrafficLights(std::vector<int>& lights) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = static_cast<int>(lights.size()) - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (lights[mid] == 0) {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(lights[low], lights[mid]);\n\t\t\tlow++;\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else if (lights[mid] == 1) {\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(lights[mid], lights[high]);\n\t\t\thigh--;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nTransitioning from an O(n^2) insertion sort to the O(n) Dutch National Flag algorithm provides a massive speedup for sorting categories like traffic lights (0, 1, 2). This eliminates the cost of repeated shifting and performs the sort in a single linear scan."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid stableSortPoints(std::vector<int>& points) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < points.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = 0; j < points.size() - 1; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (points[j] > points[j+1]) {\n\t\t\t\tstd::swap(points[j], points[j+1]);\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid stableSortPoints(std::vector<int>& points) {\n\tstd::stable_sort(points.begin(), points.end());\n}\n---\nBubble sort is O(n^2) and stable, but std::stable_sort provides the same stability with O(n log^2 n) or O(n log n) complexity depending on memory availability. This drastically improves performance while maintaining the relative order of equal elements."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <string>\n\nstruct Item {\n\tint category;\n\tint value;\n};\n\nvoid sortItems(std::vector<Item>& items) {\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < (int)items.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (int j = i + 1; j < (int)items.size(); j++) {\n\t\t\tif (items[i].category > items[j].category) {\n\t\t\t\tstd::swap(items[i], items[j]);\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstruct Item {\n\tint category;\n\tint value;\n};\n\nvoid sortItems(std::vector<Item>& items) {\n\tstd::sort(items.begin(), items.end(), [](const Item& a, const Item& b) {\n\t\treturn a.category < b.category;\n\t});\n}\n---\nReplacing the O(n^2) selection sort with std::sort improves complexity to O(n log n). By using a lambda comparator, we avoid the inefficient nested loop and take advantage of the standard library's highly tuned sorting implementation, which is significantly faster for larger collections."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortFlagColors(std::vector<int>& arr) {\n\tint n = arr.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tint m = i;\n\t\tfor (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (arr[j] < arr[m]) m = j;\n\t\t}\n\t\tstd::swap(arr[i], arr[m]);\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortFlagColors(std::vector<int>& arr) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = (int)arr.size() - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (arr[mid] == 0) {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(arr[low], arr[mid]);\n\t\t\tlow++;\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else if (arr[mid] == 1) {\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(arr[mid], arr[high]);\n\t\t\thigh--;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nThe original O(n^2) selection sort is inefficient for arrays with only three distinct values. The Dutch National Flag algorithm optimizes this to O(n) time by partitioning the array in a single pass, using three pointers to organize 0s, 1s, and 2s."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid findSmallestTen(std::vector<int>& data) {\n\tif (data.size() < 10) return;\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < data.size(); ++i) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = 0; j < data.size() - 1; ++j) {\n\t\t\tif (data[j] > data[j+1]) std::swap(data[j], data[j+1]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid findSmallestTen(std::vector<int>& data) {\n\tif (data.size() > 10) {\n\t\tstd::partial_sort(data.begin(), data.begin() + 10, data.end());\n\t}\n}\n---\nThe O(n^2) bubble sort is replaced with std::partial_sort, improving complexity to O(n log k) where k=10. This optimization is highly effective for large datasets where only a small subset of elements needs to be sorted and placed at the beginning of the container."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <string>\n\nstruct Entry {\n\tint timestamp;\n\tint type;\n};\n\nvoid sortEntries(std::vector<Entry>& entries) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < entries.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < entries.size(); j++) {\n\t\t\tif (entries[i].timestamp > entries[j].timestamp) {\n\t\t\t\tstd::swap(entries[i], entries[j]);\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstruct Entry {\n\tint timestamp;\n\tint type;\n};\n\nvoid sortEntries(std::vector<Entry>& entries) {\n\tstd::sort(entries.begin(), entries.end(), [](const Entry& a, const Entry& b) {\n\t\treturn a.timestamp < b.timestamp;\n\t});\n}\n---\nThe code is optimized from an O(n^2) selection sort to O(n log n) using std::sort. For log entries or time-series data, this scaling is critical. The use of a lambda comparator ensures that the sorting logic is inline and highly efficient for the specific struct members."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortZeroOneTwo(std::vector<int>& vec) {\n\tint n = vec.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < n - 1; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (vec[j] > vec[j+1]) {\n\t\t\t\tstd::swap(vec[j], vec[j+1]);\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortZeroOneTwo(std::vector<int>& vec) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = (int)vec.size() - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (vec[mid] == 0) {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(vec[low], vec[mid]);\n\t\t\tlow++;\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else if (vec[mid] == 1) {\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(vec[mid], vec[high]);\n\t\t\thigh--;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nReplacing the O(n^2) bubble sort with the O(n) Dutch National Flag algorithm provides a significant speedup for three-value datasets. This approach minimizes memory writes and ensures that each element is examined exactly once, which is the theoretical optimum for this problem."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <string>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstruct Student {\n\tstd::string name;\n\tint score;\n};\n\nvoid sortStudents(std::vector<Student>& list) {\n\tint n = list.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < n - i - 1; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (list[j].score < list[j + 1].score) {\n\t\t\t\tstd::swap(list[j], list[j + 1]);\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <string>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstruct Student {\n\tstd::string name;\n\tint score;\n};\n\nvoid sortStudents(std::vector<Student>& list) {\n\tstd::sort(list.begin(), list.end(), [](const Student& a, const Student& b) {\n\t\treturn a.score > b.score;\n\t});\n}\n---\nBubble sort's O(n^2) complexity is replaced by std::sort's O(n log n). For objects like Student which contain strings, reducing the number of swaps and comparisons through a more efficient algorithm significantly lowers the computational overhead and memory traffic."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortBits(std::vector<int>& arr) {\n\tint n = arr.size();\n\tfor (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tint key = arr[i];\n\t\tint j = i - 1;\n\t\twhile (j >= 0 && arr[j] > key) {\n\t\t\tarr[j + 1] = arr[j];\n\t\t\tj--;\n\t\t}\n\t\tarr[j + 1] = key;\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortBits(std::vector<int>& arr) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = (int)arr.size() - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (arr[mid] == 0) {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(arr[low], arr[mid]);\n\t\t\tlow++;\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else if (arr[mid] == 1) {\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(arr[mid], arr[high]);\n\t\t\thigh--;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nThe insertion sort is replaced with the O(n) Dutch National Flag algorithm. Even for binary arrays (0s and 1s), this approach is faster than O(n^2) alternatives because it resolves the sort in a single linear pass with a constant number of operations per element."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortFloats(std::vector<float>& vec) {\n\tint n = vec.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tfor (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (vec[i] > vec[j]) std::swap(vec[i], vec[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortFloats(std::vector<float>& vec) {\n\tstd::sort(vec.begin(), vec.end());\n}\n---\nThe O(n^2) selection sort is optimized to O(n log n) by using std::sort. The standard library implementation is highly optimized for primitive types like float, utilizing cache-efficient partitioning and avoiding the redundant comparisons inherent in a naive nested-loop sort."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortThreeState(std::vector<int>& vals) {\n\tint n = vals.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tint min_idx = i;\n\t\tfor (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (vals[j] < vals[min_idx]) min_idx = j;\n\t\t}\n\t\tint temp = vals[min_idx];\n\t\tvals[min_idx] = vals[i];\n\t\tvals[i] = temp;\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortThreeState(std::vector<int>& vals) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = (int)vals.size() - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (vals[mid] == 0) {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(vals[low], vals[mid]);\n\t\t\tlow++;\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else if (vals[mid] == 1) {\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(vals[mid], vals[high]);\n\t\t\thigh--;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nThe original O(n^2) selection sort is replaced with the O(n) Dutch National Flag algorithm. This is the most efficient method for sorting elements with only three possible keys, as it organizes the data in place with exactly one scan and minimum swaps."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid partialSortArray(std::vector<int>& vec, int k) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < vec.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < vec.size(); j++) {\n\t\t\tif (vec[i] > vec[j]) std::swap(vec[i], vec[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid partialSortArray(std::vector<int>& vec, int k) {\n\tif (k > 0 && k <= (int)vec.size()) {\n\t\tstd::partial_sort(vec.begin(), vec.begin() + k, vec.end());\n\t}\n}\n---\nThe optimization replaces a full O(n^2) selection sort with std::partial_sort, which has a complexity of O(n log k). This is far more efficient when only the first k smallest elements need to be sorted, especially as n grows large."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortColorsSinglePass(std::vector<int>& arr) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 1; i < arr.size(); i++) {\n\t\tint key = arr[i];\n\t\tint j = (int)i - 1;\n\t\twhile (j >= 0 && arr[j] > key) {\n\t\t\tarr[j + 1] = arr[j];\n\t\t\tj--;\n\t\t}\n\t\tarr[j + 1] = key;\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortColorsSinglePass(std::vector<int>& arr) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = (int)arr.size() - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (arr[mid] == 0) {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(arr[low], arr[mid]);\n\t\t\tlow++;\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else if (arr[mid] == 1) {\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(arr[mid], arr[high]);\n\t\t\thigh--;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nThe O(n^2) insertion sort is replaced by the O(n) Dutch National Flag algorithm. For sorting items with exactly three categories (0, 1, 2), this 3-way partitioning logic ensures linear time complexity and minimizes both comparisons and memory shifts."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstruct Data {\n\tint priority;\n\tint id;\n};\n\nvoid sortData(std::vector<Data>& vec) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < vec.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = 0; j < vec.size() - 1; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (vec[j].priority > vec[j+1].priority) {\n\t\t\t\tstd::swap(vec[j], vec[j+1]);\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstruct Data {\n\tint priority;\n\tint id;\n};\n\nvoid sortData(std::vector<Data>& vec) {\n\tstd::sort(vec.begin(), vec.end(), [](const Data& a, const Data& b) {\n\t\treturn a.priority < b.priority;\n\t});\n}\n---\nReplacing the O(n^2) bubble sort with std::sort improves complexity to O(n log n). This optimization significantly reduces the number of comparisons and swaps, and the use of a lambda comparator allows for efficient, custom sorting logic while maintaining high performance."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortSmallInts(std::vector<int>& nums) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tfor (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (nums[i] > nums[j]) {\n\t\t\t\tstd::swap(nums[i], nums[j]);\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortSmallInts(std::vector<int>& nums) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = (int)nums.size() - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (nums[mid] == 0) {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(nums[low], nums[mid]);\n\t\t\tlow++;\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else if (nums[mid] == 1) {\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(nums[mid], nums[high]);\n\t\t\thigh--;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nThe O(n^2) nested loop sort is optimized using the O(n) Dutch National Flag algorithm. This is the optimal linear-time solution for containers containing only three distinct values (0, 1, 2), as it performs the sort in-place with a single pass."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid getTopK(std::vector<int>& data, int k) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < data.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < data.size(); j++) {\n\t\t\tif (data[i] < data[j]) std::swap(data[i], data[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid getTopK(std::vector<int>& data, int k) {\n\tif (k > 0 && k <= (int)data.size()) {\n\t\tstd::partial_sort(data.begin(), data.begin() + k, data.end(), std::greater<int>());\n\t}\n}\n---\nThe manual O(n^2) selection sort is replaced with std::partial_sort, improving the complexity to O(n log k). This efficiently finds and sorts only the k largest elements, which is much faster than sorting the entire array when n is large and k is relatively small."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortIntegers(std::vector<int>& vec) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < vec.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = 0; j < vec.size() - 1; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (vec[j] > vec[j+1]) {\n\t\t\t\tstd::swap(vec[j], vec[j+1]);\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortIntegers(std::vector<int>& vec) {\n\tstd::sort(vec.begin(), vec.end());\n}\n---\nThe O(n^2) bubble sort is optimized to O(n log n) by using std::sort. This significantly improves performance for large arrays by leveraging Introsort's combination of quicksort, heapsort, and insertion sort, ensuring robust and fast execution on diverse input patterns."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid dnfSort(std::vector<int>& arr) {\n\tint n = arr.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tfor (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (arr[i] > arr[j]) {\n\t\t\t\tint t = arr[i];\n\t\t\t\tarr[i] = arr[j];\n\t\t\t\tarr[j] = t;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid dnfSort(std::vector<int>& arr) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = (int)arr.size() - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (arr[mid] == 0) {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(arr[low], arr[mid]);\n\t\t\tlow++;\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else if (arr[mid] == 1) {\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(arr[mid], arr[high]);\n\t\t\thigh--;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nReplacing the O(n^2) selection sort with the Dutch National Flag algorithm reduces time complexity to O(n). This is the optimal approach for arrays consisting of only three unique values, as it sorts the container in a single linear pass with minimal swaps."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <string>\n\nstruct Record {\n\tint key;\n\tstd::string data;\n};\n\nvoid sortRecords(std::vector<Record>& records) {\n\tint n = records.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < n - i - 1; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (records[j].key > records[j+1].key) {\n\t\t\t\tstd::swap(records[j], records[j+1]);\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <string>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstruct Record {\n\tint key;\n\tstd::string data;\n};\n\nvoid sortRecords(std::vector<Record>& records) {\n\tstd::sort(records.begin(), records.end(), [](const Record& a, const Record& b) {\n\t\treturn a.key < b.key;\n\t});\n}\n---\nBubble sort is O(n^2), while std::sort is O(n log n). For structs containing strings, this optimization is vital because it drastically reduces the number of object moves and comparisons, which are expensive due to the dynamic memory management within std::string."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid findFifthSmallest(std::vector<int>& nums) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tfor (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (nums[i] > nums[j]) std::swap(nums[i], nums[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid findFifthSmallest(std::vector<int>& nums) {\n\tif (nums.size() > 5) {\n\t\tstd::nth_element(nums.begin(), nums.begin() + 4, nums.end());\n\t}\n}\n---\nInstead of an O(n^2) selection sort, std::nth_element is used to find the 5th smallest element in O(n) average time. This is much faster for large arrays because it avoids sorting the entire collection, only doing enough work to partition the k-th element."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortTripleValues(std::vector<int>& arr) {\n\tint n = arr.size();\n\tfor (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tint k = arr[i];\n\t\tint j = i - 1;\n\t\twhile (j >= 0 && arr[j] > k) {\n\t\t\tarr[j + 1] = arr[j];\n\t\t\tj--;\n\t\t}\n\t\tarr[j + 1] = k;\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortTripleValues(std::vector<int>& arr) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = (int)arr.size() - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (arr[mid] == 0) {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(arr[low], arr[mid]);\n\t\t\tlow++;\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else if (arr[mid] == 1) {\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(arr[mid], arr[high]);\n\t\t\thigh--;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nThe O(n^2) insertion sort is replaced with the O(n) Dutch National Flag algorithm. For arrays with only three distinct values (0, 1, 2), this 3-way partition is the optimal way to sort in a single linear pass with constant space and minimal swaps."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortDoubleVector(std::vector<double>& v) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < v.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = 0; j < v.size() - 1; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (v[j] > v[j+1]) std::swap(v[j], v[j+1]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortDoubleVector(std::vector<double>& v) {\n\tstd::sort(v.begin(), v.end());\n}\n---\nThe O(n^2) bubble sort is replaced by std::sort, improving the complexity to O(n log n). This leverages the highly tuned introsort implementation in the C++ standard library, which is significantly more efficient than manual sorting for large containers of primitive doubles."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <string>\n\nstruct Employee {\n\tint salary;\n\tint age;\n};\n\nvoid sortEmployees(std::vector<Employee>& emps) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < emps.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < emps.size(); j++) {\n\t\t\tif (emps[i].salary < emps[j].salary) {\n\t\t\t\tstd::swap(emps[i], emps[j]);\n\t\t\t} else if (emps[i].salary == emps[j].salary && emps[i].age < emps[j].age) {\n\t\t\t\tstd::swap(emps[i], emps[j]);\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstruct Employee {\n\tint salary;\n\tint age;\n};\n\nvoid sortEmployees(std::vector<Employee>& emps) {\n\tstd::sort(emps.begin(), emps.end(), [](const Employee& a, const Employee& b) {\n\t\tif (a.salary != b.salary) return a.salary > b.salary;\n\t\treturn a.age > b.age;\n\t});\n}\n---\nThe manual selection sort (O(n^2)) is replaced with std::sort (O(n log n)) and a lambda comparator. This is not only faster but also much more readable and maintainable for multi-level sorting logic, efficiently handling the large number of comparisons required for employee records."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sort012(std::vector<int>& a) {\n\tint n = a.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tint m = i;\n\t\tfor (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (a[j] < a[m]) m = j;\n\t\t}\n\t\tint t = a[i];\n\t\ta[i] = a[m];\n\t\ta[m] = t;\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sort012(std::vector<int>& a) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = (int)a.size() - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (a[mid] == 0) {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(a[low], a[mid]);\n\t\t\tlow++;\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else if (a[mid] == 1) {\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(a[mid], a[high]);\n\t\t\thigh--;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nThe O(n^2) selection sort is optimized using the O(n) Dutch National Flag algorithm. This single-pass partitioning method is specifically designed for three-value sorts, providing the best possible performance by reducing comparisons and data movement to a linear scan."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid topFivePercent(std::vector<int>& scores) {\n\tint n = scores.size();\n\tint k = n / 20;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tfor (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (scores[i] < scores[j]) std::swap(scores[i], scores[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid topFivePercent(std::vector<int>& scores) {\n\tint n = (int)scores.size();\n\tint k = n / 20;\n\tif (k > 0 && k <= n) {\n\t\tstd::partial_sort(scores.begin(), scores.begin() + k, scores.end(), std::greater<int>());\n\t}\n}\n---\nThe O(n^2) sort is optimized to O(n log k) using std::partial_sort. This significantly speeds up the process of finding the top percentile of scores, as it avoids the unnecessary overhead of sorting the majority of the dataset that falls outside the top k elements."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortNearlySortedSmall(std::vector<int>& vec) {\n\tint n = vec.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < n - 1; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (vec[j] > vec[j+1]) std::swap(vec[j], vec[j+1]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortNearlySortedSmall(std::vector<int>& vec) {\n\tstd::sort(vec.begin(), vec.end());\n}\n---\nReplacing an O(n^2) bubble sort with std::sort (O(n log n)) improves performance significantly. Standard library sorts are often adaptive and can detect partially sorted ranges, making them highly efficient even for data that is nearly in order, while avoiding the worst-case behavior of naive loops."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortThreeClasses(std::vector<int>& classes) {\n\tint n = classes.size();\n\tfor (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tint val = classes[i];\n\t\tint j = i - 1;\n\t\twhile (j >= 0 && classes[j] > val) {\n\t\t\tclasses[j + 1] = classes[j];\n\t\t\tj--;\n\t\t}\n\t\tclasses[j + 1] = val;\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortThreeClasses(std::vector<int>& classes) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = (int)classes.size() - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (classes[mid] == 0) {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(classes[low], classes[mid]);\n\t\t\tlow++;\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else if (classes[mid] == 1) {\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(classes[mid], classes[high]);\n\t\t\thigh--;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nThe original insertion sort is O(n^2), which is inefficient for datasets with limited unique keys. Using the O(n) Dutch National Flag algorithm partitions the data in place in a single linear pass, resulting in an optimal solution for three-category sorting tasks."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <string>\n\nstruct Car {\n\tint year;\n\tint price;\n};\n\nvoid sortCars(std::vector<Car>& cars) {\n\tint n = (int)cars.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tint best = i;\n\t\tfor (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (cars[j].year > cars[best].year) best = j;\n\t\t\telse if (cars[j].year == cars[best].year && cars[j].price < cars[best].price) best = j;\n\t\t}\n\t\tstd::swap(cars[i], cars[best]);\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstruct Car {\n\tint year;\n\tint price;\n};\n\nvoid sortCars(std::vector<Car>& cars) {\n\tstd::sort(cars.begin(), cars.end(), [](const Car& a, const Car& b) {\n\t\tif (a.year != b.year) return a.year > b.year;\n\t\treturn a.price < b.price;\n\t});\n}\n---\nReplacing the O(n^2) selection sort with std::sort (O(n log n)) and a lambda comparator provides a much more efficient way to sort by multiple fields. This eliminates redundant comparisons and utilizes an optimized introsort that is faster for large car inventory datasets."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortLongs(std::vector<long long>& nums) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < nums.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = 0; j < nums.size() - 1; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (nums[j] > nums[j+1]) std::swap(nums[j], nums[j+1]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortLongs(std::vector<long long>& nums) {\n\tstd::sort(nums.begin(), nums.end());\n}\n---\nThe O(n^2) bubble sort is optimized to O(n log n) by using std::sort. For 64-bit long long integers, the standard library's introsort is particularly effective as it minimizes comparisons and uses cache-friendly partitioning to handle large volumes of data efficiently."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortThreePriorities(std::vector<int>& items) {\n\tint n = items.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tfor (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (items[i] > items[j]) {\n\t\t\t\tint tmp = items[i];\n\t\t\t\titems[i] = items[j];\n\t\t\t\titems[j] = tmp;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortThreePriorities(std::vector<int>& items) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = (int)items.size() - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (items[mid] == 0) {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(items[low], items[mid]);\n\t\t\tlow++;\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else if (items[mid] == 1) {\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(items[mid], items[high]);\n\t\t\thigh--;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nThe manual selection sort (O(n^2)) is replaced with the Dutch National Flag algorithm (O(n)). This is the ideal optimization for sorting lists with only three distinct priority levels, as it organizes the entire vector in a single linear pass with constant extra space."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid topThreePercent(std::vector<int>& values) {\n\tint n = values.size();\n\tint k = (n * 3) / 100;\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < n - 1; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (values[j] < values[j+1]) std::swap(values[j], values[j+1]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid topThreePercent(std::vector<int>& values) {\n\tint n = (int)values.size();\n\tint k = (n * 3) / 100;\n\tif (k > 0 && k <= n) {\n\t\tstd::partial_sort(values.begin(), values.begin() + k, values.end(), std::greater<int>());\n\t}\n}\n---\nThe code is optimized from O(n^2) bubble sort to O(n log k) using std::partial_sort. This significantly improves performance when extracting a small fraction (3%) of the highest values from a large dataset, as it avoids sorting the entire collection of values."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <string>\n\nstruct Document {\n\tint length;\n\tint id;\n};\n\nvoid sortDocs(std::vector<Document>& docs) {\n\tint n = docs.size();\n\tfor (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tDocument key = docs[i];\n\t\tint j = i - 1;\n\t\twhile (j >= 0 && docs[j].length > key.length) {\n\t\t\tdocs[j + 1] = docs[j];\n\t\t\tj--;\n\t\t}\n\t\tdocs[j + 1] = key;\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstruct Document {\n\tint length;\n\tint id;\n};\n\nvoid sortDocs(std::vector<Document>& docs) {\n\tstd::sort(docs.begin(), docs.end(), [](const Document& a, const Document& b) {\n\t\treturn a.length < b.length;\n\t});\n}\n---\nThe O(n^2) insertion sort is replaced with std::sort, achieving O(n log n) complexity. For sorting structs like Document, the standard library sort is much more efficient at managing comparisons and memory movements, particularly when the number of documents is substantial."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortTriples(std::vector<int>& data) {\n\tint n = data.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tfor (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (data[i] > data[j]) {\n\t\t\t\tint temp = data[i];\n\t\t\t\tdata[i] = data[j];\n\t\t\t\tdata[j] = temp;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortTriples(std::vector<int>& data) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = (int)data.size() - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (data[mid] == 0) {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(data[low], data[mid]);\n\t\t\tlow++;\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else if (data[mid] == 1) {\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(data[mid], data[high]);\n\t\t\thigh--;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nReplacing the O(n^2) selection sort with the Dutch National Flag algorithm reduces the complexity to O(n). This provides the fastest possible sort for a container with three unique values (0, 1, 2) by organizing the elements in place during a single pass."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid findNthElement(std::vector<int>& arr, int n_idx) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < arr.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = i + 1; j < arr.size(); j++) {\n\t\t\tif (arr[i] > arr[j]) std::swap(arr[i], arr[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid findNthElement(std::vector<int>& arr, int n_idx) {\n\tif (n_idx >= 0 && n_idx < (int)arr.size()) {\n\t\tstd::nth_element(arr.begin(), arr.begin() + n_idx, arr.end());\n\t}\n}\n---\nThe original O(n^2) selection sort is optimized to O(n) average time using std::nth_element. This is far more efficient when only a specific position in the sorted order is required, as it avoids the computational cost of fully sorting the entire array."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <string>\n\nstruct RecordItem {\n\tint rank;\n\tdouble value;\n};\n\nvoid sortRecordItems(std::vector<RecordItem>& items) {\n\tint n = items.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < n - 1; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (items[j].rank < items[j+1].rank) {\n\t\t\t\tstd::swap(items[j], items[j+1]);\n\t\t\t} else if (items[j].rank == items[j+1].rank && items[j].value > items[j+1].value) {\n\t\t\t\tstd::swap(items[j], items[j+1]);\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstruct RecordItem {\n\tint rank;\n\tdouble value;\n};\n\nvoid sortRecordItems(std::vector<RecordItem>& items) {\n\tstd::sort(items.begin(), items.end(), [](const RecordItem& a, const RecordItem& b) {\n\t\tif (a.rank != b.rank) return a.rank > b.rank;\n\t\treturn a.value < b.value;\n\t});\n}\n---\nThe O(n^2) bubble sort is optimized to O(n log n) using std::sort with a lambda. This is significantly faster for sorting structs by multiple fields, as it utilizes highly efficient internal partitioning and reduces the total number of swaps and comparisons needed to order the data."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortSignals(std::vector<int>& signals) {\n\tint n = signals.size();\n\tfor (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tint curr = signals[i];\n\t\tint j = i - 1;\n\t\twhile (j >= 0 && signals[j] > curr) {\n\t\t\tsignals[j + 1] = signals[j];\n\t\t\tj--;\n\t\t}\n\t\tsignals[j + 1] = curr;\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortSignals(std::vector<int>& signals) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = (int)signals.size() - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (signals[mid] == 0) {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(signals[low], signals[mid]);\n\t\t\tlow++;\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else if (signals[mid] == 1) {\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(signals[mid], signals[high]);\n\t\t\thigh--;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nReplacing the O(n^2) insertion sort with the O(n) Dutch National Flag algorithm provides a linear-time solution for sorting signal data with only three states (0, 1, 2). This optimization is much more efficient as it processes each element only once and minimizes data shifting."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortChars(std::vector<char>& chars) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < chars.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = 0; j < chars.size() - 1; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (chars[j] > chars[j+1]) std::swap(chars[j], chars[j+1]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortChars(std::vector<char>& chars) {\n\tstd::sort(chars.begin(), chars.end());\n}\n---\nThe O(n^2) bubble sort is replaced with std::sort, improving the complexity to O(n log n). This is particularly effective for small primitive types like char, where the standard library can use optimized introsort to achieve extremely high throughput compared to manual loop-based sorting."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <string>\n\nstruct Player {\n\tstd::string name;\n\tint level;\n};\n\nvoid sortPlayers(std::vector<Player>& players) {\n\tint n = players.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tint min_idx = i;\n\t\tfor (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (players[j].level < players[min_idx].level) min_idx = j;\n\t\t}\n\t\tstd::swap(players[i], players[min_idx]);\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <string>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstruct Player {\n\tstd::string name;\n\tint level;\n};\n\nvoid sortPlayers(std::vector<Player>& players) {\n\tstd::sort(players.begin(), players.end(), [](const Player& a, const Player& b) {\n\t\treturn a.level < b.level;\n\t});\n}\n---\nSelection sort's O(n^2) complexity is replaced with std::sort's O(n log n). For Player objects with strings, reducing comparisons and swaps using the efficient introsort implementation in the standard library significantly lowers the processing time and overhead of memory operations."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortEnumValues(std::vector<int>& data) {\n\tint n = data.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tfor (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (data[i] > data[j]) std::swap(data[i], data[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortEnumValues(std::vector<int>& data) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = (int)data.size() - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (data[mid] == 0) {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(data[low], data[mid]);\n\t\t\tlow++;\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else if (data[mid] == 1) {\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(data[mid], data[high]);\n\t\t\thigh--;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nReplacing an O(n^2) selection sort with the Dutch National Flag algorithm (O(n)) provides a massive performance boost for datasets consisting of only three unique enum values (0, 1, 2). This linear-time solution organizes the data in place with just a single scan."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid findTopTenScores(std::vector<int>& scores) {\n\tint n = scores.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < n - 1; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (scores[j] < scores[j+1]) std::swap(scores[j], scores[j+1]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid findTopTenScores(std::vector<int>& scores) {\n\tif (scores.size() > 10) {\n\t\tstd::partial_sort(scores.begin(), scores.begin() + 10, scores.end(), std::greater<int>());\n\t}\n}\n---\nThe O(n^2) bubble sort is replaced by std::partial_sort, which has a complexity of O(n log k) where k=10. This is highly efficient when only the top few scores are needed, as it avoids the massive amount of extra work required to sort the entire list."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <string>\n\nstruct FileInfo {\n\tint size;\n\tstd::string name;\n};\n\nvoid sortFiles(std::vector<FileInfo>& files) {\n\tint n = files.size();\n\tfor (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tFileInfo k = files[i];\n\t\tint j = i - 1;\n\t\twhile (j >= 0 && files[j].size > k.size) {\n\t\t\tfiles[j + 1] = files[j];\n\t\t\tj--;\n\t\t}\n\t\tfiles[j + 1] = k;\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstruct FileInfo {\n\tint size;\n\tstd::string name;\n};\n\nvoid sortFiles(std::vector<FileInfo>& files) {\n\tstd::sort(files.begin(), files.end(), [](const FileInfo& a, const FileInfo& b) {\n\t\treturn a.size < b.size;\n\t});\n}\n---\nThe O(n^2) insertion sort is replaced with std::sort, improving the complexity to O(n log n). This is a more scalable approach for sorting file information by size, as it leverages the highly optimized internal logic of the standard library and reduces the total number of expensive object reassignments."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortFlagsLinear(std::vector<int>& v) {\n\tint n = v.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tfor (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (v[i] > v[j]) std::swap(v[i], v[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortFlagsLinear(std::vector<int>& v) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = (int)v.size() - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (v[mid] == 0) {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(v[low], v[mid]);\n\t\t\tlow++;\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else if (v[mid] == 1) {\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(v[mid], v[high]);\n\t\t\thigh--;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nReplacing the O(n^2) selection sort with the Dutch National Flag algorithm optimizes the sort to O(n). This provides a linear-time, in-place sort for arrays containing only three distinct values (0, 1, 2) by organizing them using a single efficient pass over the container."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortUnsigned(std::vector<unsigned int>& v) {\n\tfor (size_t i = 0; i < v.size(); i++) {\n\t\tfor (size_t j = 0; j < v.size() - 1; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (v[j] > v[j+1]) std::swap(v[j], v[j+1]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortUnsigned(std::vector<unsigned int>& v) {\n\tstd::sort(v.begin(), v.end());\n}\n---\nThe O(n^2) bubble sort is replaced with std::sort, achieving O(n log n) complexity. Standard library sorts are exceptionally fast for unsigned integer types, using low-level optimizations and efficient partitioning that far outperform a naive nested-loop implementation as the vector size grows."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <string>\n\nstruct ItemData {\n\tint prio;\n\tstd::string tag;\n};\n\nvoid sortItemData(std::vector<ItemData>& vec) {\n\tint n = vec.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tint idx = i;\n\t\tfor (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (vec[j].prio < vec[idx].prio) idx = j;\n\t\t\telse if (vec[j].prio == vec[idx].prio && vec[j].tag < vec[idx].tag) idx = j;\n\t\t}\n\t\tstd::swap(vec[i], vec[idx]);\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstruct ItemData {\n\tint prio;\n\tstd::string tag;\n};\n\nvoid sortItemData(std::vector<ItemData>& vec) {\n\tstd::sort(vec.begin(), vec.end(), [](const ItemData& a, const ItemData& b) {\n\t\tif (a.prio != b.prio) return a.prio < b.prio;\n\t\treturn a.tag < b.tag;\n\t});\n}\n---\nSelection sort's O(n^2) complexity is replaced by std::sort's O(n log n) with a custom lambda. This optimization is far more efficient for sorting structs by multiple fields, especially when one of the fields is a string, as it minimizes the total number of comparisons and data movements."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid findTopFive(std::vector<int>& v) {\n\tint n = v.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tfor (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (v[i] < v[j]) std::swap(v[i], v[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid findTopFive(std::vector<int>& v) {\n\tif (v.size() > 5) {\n\t\tstd::partial_sort(v.begin(), v.begin() + 5, v.end(), std::greater<int>());\n\t}\n}\n---\nReplacing the O(n^2) selection sort with std::partial_sort reduces complexity to O(n log k) where k=5. This avoids the excessive work of sorting the entire array when only the five largest elements are required at the front, significantly improving performance for large data sets."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortCategoryData(std::vector<int>& arr) {\n\tint n = arr.size();\n\tfor (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tint k = arr[i];\n\t\tint j = i - 1;\n\t\twhile (j >= 0 && arr[j] > k) {\n\t\t\tarr[j + 1] = arr[j];\n\t\t\tj--;\n\t\t}\n\t\tarr[j + 1] = k;\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortCategoryData(std::vector<int>& arr) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = (int)arr.size() - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (arr[mid] == 0) {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(arr[low], arr[mid]);\n\t\t\tlow++;\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else if (arr[mid] == 1) {\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(arr[mid], arr[high]);\n\t\t\thigh--;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nThe O(n^2) insertion sort is replaced with the O(n) Dutch National Flag algorithm. This provides an optimal linear-time solution for sorting categories (represented as 0, 1, and 2) in a single pass, which is significantly faster and uses less memory traffic than traditional sorting algorithms."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortIntegersBubble(std::vector<int>& data) {\n\tint n = data.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < n - i - 1; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (data[j] > data[j+1]) std::swap(data[j], data[j+1]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortIntegersBubble(std::vector<int>& data) {\n\tstd::sort(data.begin(), data.end());\n}\n---\nThe O(n^2) bubble sort is replaced with std::sort, which provides O(n log n) average and worst-case performance. This optimization uses a highly tuned introsort implementation that is much more efficient at managing comparisons and memory access for large integer arrays."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <string>\n\nstruct UserProfile {\n\tint score;\n\tint id;\n};\n\nvoid sortProfiles(std::vector<UserProfile>& list) {\n\tint n = list.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tfor (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (list[i].score < list[j].score) {\n\t\t\t\tstd::swap(list[i], list[j]);\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstruct UserProfile {\n\tint score;\n\tint id;\n};\n\nvoid sortProfiles(std::vector<UserProfile>& list) {\n\tstd::sort(list.begin(), list.end(), [](const UserProfile& a, const UserProfile& b) {\n\t\treturn a.score > b.score;\n\t});\n}\n---\nSelection sort's O(n^2) complexity is replaced with std::sort's O(n log n). By using a lambda comparator to sort by profile score, we leverage the standard library's efficient implementation to handle large user lists with significantly better performance and cleaner code."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortBinaryValues(std::vector<int>& vec) {\n\tint n = (int)vec.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tfor (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (vec[i] > vec[j]) std::swap(vec[i], vec[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortBinaryValues(std::vector<int>& vec) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = (int)vec.size() - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (vec[mid] == 0) {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(vec[low], vec[mid]);\n\t\t\tlow++;\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else if (vec[mid] == 1) {\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(vec[mid], vec[high]);\n\t\t\thigh--;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nReplacing the O(n^2) selection sort with the O(n) Dutch National Flag algorithm provides a linear-time solution for containers with three distinct values (0, 1, 2). This optimization is much more efficient than generic sorts, as it organizes the data in place using a single pass."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid findMedianQuick(std::vector<int>& arr) {\n\tint n = arr.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tfor (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (arr[i] > arr[j]) std::swap(arr[i], arr[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid findMedianQuick(std::vector<int>& arr) {\n\tif (!arr.empty()) {\n\t\tstd::nth_element(arr.begin(), arr.begin() + arr.size() / 2, arr.end());\n\t}\n}\n---\nInstead of an O(n^2) selection sort, std::nth_element is used to find the median in O(n) average time. This avoids the cost of fully sorting the array, making it significantly faster for large datasets where only the median value is required."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <string>\n\nstruct City {\n\tstd::string name;\n\tint pop;\n};\n\nvoid sortCities(std::vector<City>& cities) {\n\tint n = cities.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < n - i - 1; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (cities[j].pop < cities[j + 1].pop) {\n\t\t\t\tstd::swap(cities[j], cities[j + 1]);\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <string>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstruct City {\n\tstd::string name;\n\tint pop;\n};\n\nvoid sortCities(std::vector<City>& cities) {\n\tstd::sort(cities.begin(), cities.end(), [](const City& a, const City& b) {\n\t\treturn a.pop > b.pop;\n\t});\n}\n---\nBubble sort's O(n^2) complexity is replaced with std::sort's O(n log n). For City objects containing strings, this optimization significantly reduces the overhead from multiple swaps and comparisons, leveraging the standard library's efficient implementation for much faster sorting of large lists."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortThreeDigit(std::vector<int>& nums) {\n\tint n = nums.size();\n\tfor (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tint key = nums[i];\n\t\tint j = i - 1;\n\t\twhile (j >= 0 && nums[j] > key) {\n\t\t\tnums[j + 1] = nums[j];\n\t\t\tj--;\n\t\t}\n\t\tnums[j + 1] = key;\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortThreeDigit(std::vector<int>& nums) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = (int)nums.size() - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (nums[mid] == 0) {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(nums[low], nums[mid]);\n\t\t\tlow++;\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else if (nums[mid] == 1) {\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(nums[mid], nums[high]);\n\t\t\thigh--;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nReplacing the O(n^2) insertion sort with the Dutch National Flag algorithm optimizes the sort to O(n). This is the ideal approach for arrays containing only three unique integers (0, 1, 2), as it organizes the container in a single linear pass with minimal memory operations."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortFloatsBubble(std::vector<float>& vec) {\n\tint n = vec.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < n - 1; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (vec[j] > vec[j+1]) std::swap(vec[j], vec[j+1]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortFloatsBubble(std::vector<float>& vec) {\n\tstd::sort(vec.begin(), vec.end());\n}\n---\nThe O(n^2) bubble sort is replaced by std::sort, improving complexity to O(n log n). This leverages the highly optimized introsort in the C++ standard library, which is much more efficient for large collections of floating-point numbers by reducing redundant comparisons and data movement."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <string>\n\nstruct Transaction {\n\tint amount;\n\tint time;\n};\n\nvoid sortTransactions(std::vector<Transaction>& txs) {\n\tint n = txs.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tint min_idx = i;\n\t\tfor (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (txs[j].amount < txs[min_idx].amount) min_idx = j;\n\t\t}\n\t\tstd::swap(txs[i], txs[min_idx]);\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstruct Transaction {\n\tint amount;\n\tint time;\n};\n\nvoid sortTransactions(std::vector<Transaction>& txs) {\n\tstd::sort(txs.begin(), txs.end(), [](const Transaction& a, const Transaction& b) {\n\t\treturn a.amount < b.amount;\n\t});\n}\n---\nSelection sort's O(n^2) complexity is optimized to O(n log n) by using std::sort with a lambda comparator. This provides a much more efficient way to sort transactions by amount, significantly speeding up processing for large datasets and utilizing the robust sorting logic of the standard library."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortThreeLevels(std::vector<int>& data) {\n\tint n = data.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tfor (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (data[i] > data[j]) std::swap(data[i], data[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortThreeLevels(std::vector<int>& data) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = (int)data.size() - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (data[mid] == 0) {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(data[low], data[mid]);\n\t\t\tlow++;\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else if (data[mid] == 1) {\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(data[mid], data[high]);\n\t\t\thigh--;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nThe O(n^2) selection sort is replaced with the O(n) Dutch National Flag algorithm. This single-pass sorting method is the most efficient solution for containers with only three unique values, as it partitions the data in place with minimal swaps and comparisons."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid findTopTwo(std::vector<int>& vec) {\n\tint n = vec.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < n - 1; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (vec[j] < vec[j+1]) std::swap(vec[j], vec[j+1]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid findTopTwo(std::vector<int>& vec) {\n\tif (vec.size() > 2) {\n\t\tstd::partial_sort(vec.begin(), vec.begin() + 2, vec.end(), std::greater<int>());\n\t}\n}\n---\nReplacing the O(n^2) bubble sort with std::partial_sort improves the complexity to O(n log k) where k=2. This optimization is highly effective for large datasets because it only sorts the necessary elements at the top of the vector, avoiding the substantial overhead of a complete sort."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <string>\n\nstruct SensorData {\n\tint value;\n\tint id;\n};\n\nvoid sortSensors(std::vector<SensorData>& sensors) {\n\tint n = sensors.size();\n\tfor (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tSensorData key = sensors[i];\n\t\tint j = i - 1;\n\t\twhile (j >= 0 && sensors[j].value > key.value) {\n\t\t\tsensors[j + 1] = sensors[j];\n\t\t\tj--;\n\t\t}\n\t\tsensors[j + 1] = key;\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstruct SensorData {\n\tint value;\n\tint id;\n};\n\nvoid sortSensors(std::vector<SensorData>& sensors) {\n\tstd::sort(sensors.begin(), sensors.end(), [](const SensorData& a, const SensorData& b) {\n\t\treturn a.value < b.value;\n\t});\n}\n---\nInsertion sort's O(n^2) complexity is optimized to O(n log n) using std::sort. For sensor data lists that can be very long, this provides a massive performance gain and leverages the standard library's robust, well-tested sorting implementation for improved reliability and speed."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortThreeKeys(std::vector<int>& arr) {\n\tint n = arr.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tint best = i;\n\t\tfor (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (arr[j] < arr[best]) best = j;\n\t\t}\n\t\tstd::swap(arr[i], arr[best]);\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortThreeKeys(std::vector<int>& arr) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = (int)arr.size() - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (arr[mid] == 0) {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(arr[low], arr[mid]);\n\t\t\tlow++;\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else if (arr[mid] == 1) {\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(arr[mid], arr[high]);\n\t\t\thigh--;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nSelection sort's O(n^2) complexity is replaced with the O(n) Dutch National Flag algorithm. This linear-time approach is specifically optimized for datasets with three distinct values, as it organizes the entire collection in place using a single pass with minimal data movement."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortLongsBubble(std::vector<long>& data) {\n\tint n = data.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < n - 1; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (data[j] > data[j+1]) std::swap(data[j], data[j+1]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortLongsBubble(std::vector<long>& data) {\n\tstd::sort(data.begin(), data.end());\n}\n---\nReplacing the O(n^2) bubble sort with std::sort (O(n log n)) provides a significant performance boost. Standard library sorts are highly optimized for primitive types like long, utilizing efficient partitioning and cache-friendly access patterns that are much faster than manual loop-based approaches."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <string>\n\nstruct Book {\n\tint id;\n\tint rating;\n};\n\nvoid sortBooks(std::vector<Book>& books) {\n\tint n = books.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tfor (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (books[i].rating < books[j].rating) {\n\t\t\t\tstd::swap(books[i], books[j]);\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstruct Book {\n\tint id;\n\tint rating;\n};\n\nvoid sortBooks(std::vector<Book>& books) {\n\tstd::sort(books.begin(), books.end(), [](const Book& a, const Book& b) {\n\t\treturn a.rating > b.rating;\n\t});\n}\n---\nSelection sort's O(n^2) complexity is optimized to O(n log n) using std::sort with a lambda. This is a much faster and more scalable way to sort book records by rating, leveraging the highly efficient introsort implementation provided by the C++ standard library."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortThreeStateValues(std::vector<int>& v) {\n\tint n = v.size();\n\tfor (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tint x = v[i];\n\t\tint j = i - 1;\n\t\twhile (j >= 0 && v[j] > x) {\n\t\t\tv[j + 1] = v[j];\n\t\t\tj--;\n\t\t}\n\t\tv[j + 1] = x;\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortThreeStateValues(std::vector<int>& v) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = (int)v.size() - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (v[mid] == 0) {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(v[low], v[mid]);\n\t\t\tlow++;\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else if (v[mid] == 1) {\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(v[mid], v[high]);\n\t\t\thigh--;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nInsertion sort's O(n^2) complexity is replaced by the O(n) Dutch National Flag algorithm. This provides the most efficient possible sorting for data with three categories (0, 1, 2) by partitioning the entire collection in a single pass with minimal comparison and data movement."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid findNthSmall(std::vector<int>& data, int n) {\n\tint len = data.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < len; i++) {\n\t\tfor (int j = i + 1; j < len; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (data[i] > data[j]) std::swap(data[i], data[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid findNthSmall(std::vector<int>& data, int n) {\n\tif (n >= 0 && n < (int)data.size()) {\n\t\tstd::nth_element(data.begin(), data.begin() + n, data.end());\n\t}\n}\n---\nReplacing the O(n^2) selection sort with std::nth_element reduces the average time complexity to O(n). This optimization is significantly faster because it only partitions the array enough to put the target element in its correct place, skipping the work required for a complete sort."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <string>\n\nstruct ProductRecord {\n\tint category;\n\tint price;\n};\n\nvoid sortProductRecords(std::vector<ProductRecord>& list) {\n\tint n = list.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tfor (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (list[i].category > list[j].category) {\n\t\t\t\tstd::swap(list[i], list[j]);\n\t\t\t} else if (list[i].category == list[j].category && list[i].price < list[j].price) {\n\t\t\t\tstd::swap(list[i], list[j]);\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstruct ProductRecord {\n\tint category;\n\tint price;\n};\n\nvoid sortProductRecords(std::vector<ProductRecord>& list) {\n\tstd::sort(list.begin(), list.end(), [](const ProductRecord& a, const ProductRecord& b) {\n\t\tif (a.category != b.category) return a.category < b.category;\n\t\treturn a.price > b.price;\n\t});\n}\n---\nSelection sort's O(n^2) complexity is optimized to O(n log n) using std::sort with a custom lambda. This efficiently handles multi-field sorting of product records, significantly reducing the number of comparisons and swaps compared to a manual nested-loop implementation."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortThreeDigitArray(std::vector<int>& arr) {\n\tint n = arr.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < n - 1; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (arr[j] > arr[j+1]) {\n\t\t\t\tint t = arr[j];\n\t\t\t\tarr[j] = arr[j+1];\n\t\t\t\tarr[j+1] = t;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortThreeDigitArray(std::vector<int>& arr) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = (int)arr.size() - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (arr[mid] == 0) {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(arr[low], arr[mid]);\n\t\t\tlow++;\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else if (arr[mid] == 1) {\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(arr[mid], arr[high]);\n\t\t\thigh--;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nBubble sort's O(n^2) complexity is replaced by the O(n) Dutch National Flag algorithm. This optimization provides a massive speedup for datasets containing only three unique values (0, 1, 2) by organizing the data in a single pass with minimal comparisons and memory traffic."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortShortsBubble(std::vector<short>& vec) {\n\tint n = vec.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < n - 1; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (vec[j] > vec[j+1]) std::swap(vec[j], vec[j+1]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortShortsBubble(std::vector<short>& vec) {\n\tstd::sort(vec.begin(), vec.end());\n}\n---\nReplacing the O(n^2) bubble sort with std::sort (O(n log n)) improves the performance significantly. For small primitive types like short, the standard library sort is extremely efficient, utilizing cache-friendly partitioning and low-level optimizations that are not present in naive loop-based sorts."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <string>\n\nstruct CustomerRecord {\n\tint loyalty_points;\n\tint id;\n};\n\nvoid sortCustomers(std::vector<CustomerRecord>& list) {\n\tint n = list.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tint min_idx = i;\n\t\tfor (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (list[j].loyalty_points > list[min_idx].loyalty_points) min_idx = j;\n\t\t}\n\t\tstd::swap(list[i], list[min_idx]);\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstruct CustomerRecord {\n\tint loyalty_points;\n\tint id;\n};\n\nvoid sortCustomers(std::vector<CustomerRecord>& list) {\n\tstd::sort(list.begin(), list.end(), [](const CustomerRecord& a, const CustomerRecord& b) {\n\t\treturn a.loyalty_points > b.loyalty_points;\n\t});\n}\n---\nSelection sort's O(n^2) complexity is replaced by std::sort's O(n log n). Using a lambda comparator to sort customers by loyalty points is much more efficient and scalable, leveraging the robust sorting logic of the C++ standard library for superior performance on large customer lists."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortThreeClassesArray(std::vector<int>& arr) {\n\tint n = arr.size();\n\tfor (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tint key = arr[i];\n\t\tint j = i - 1;\n\t\twhile (j >= 0 && arr[j] > key) {\n\t\t\tarr[j + 1] = arr[j];\n\t\t\tj--;\n\t\t}\n\t\tarr[j + 1] = key;\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortThreeClassesArray(std::vector<int>& arr) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = (int)arr.size() - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (arr[mid] == 0) {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(arr[low], arr[mid]);\n\t\t\tlow++;\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else if (arr[mid] == 1) {\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(arr[mid], arr[high]);\n\t\t\thigh--;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nInsertion sort's O(n^2) complexity is replaced with the O(n) Dutch National Flag algorithm. This optimization provides a linear-time sorting solution for arrays containing only three unique values (0, 1, 2) by organizing them in a single pass with minimal swaps and memory traffic."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid findNthLarge(std::vector<int>& vec, int n) {\n\tint size = vec.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {\n\t\tfor (int j = i + 1; j < size; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (vec[i] < vec[j]) std::swap(vec[i], vec[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid findNthLarge(std::vector<int>& vec, int n) {\n\tif (n >= 0 && n < (int)vec.size()) {\n\t\tstd::nth_element(vec.begin(), vec.begin() + n, vec.end(), std::greater<int>());\n\t}\n}\n---\nReplacing the O(n^2) selection sort with std::nth_element reduces the average complexity to O(n). This is much more efficient because it only partitions the array enough to place the target n-th largest element correctly, avoiding the unnecessary work of a full sort."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <string>\n\nstruct OrderItem {\n\tint quantity;\n\tint price;\n};\n\nvoid sortOrderItems(std::vector<OrderItem>& list) {\n\tint n = list.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tfor (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (list[i].quantity < list[j].quantity) {\n\t\t\t\tstd::swap(list[i], list[j]);\n\t\t\t} else if (list[i].quantity == list[j].quantity && list[i].price > list[j].price) {\n\t\t\t\tstd::swap(list[i], list[j]);\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstruct OrderItem {\n\tint quantity;\n\tint price;\n};\n\nvoid sortOrderItems(std::vector<OrderItem>& list) {\n\tstd::sort(list.begin(), list.end(), [](const OrderItem& a, const OrderItem& b) {\n\t\tif (a.quantity != b.quantity) return a.quantity > b.quantity;\n\t\treturn a.price < b.price;\n\t});\n}\n---\nSelection sort's O(n^2) complexity is replaced with std::sort's O(n log n). Using a lambda comparator for multi-field sorting of order items is much more efficient and scalable, leveraging the highly optimized introsort implementation provided by the standard library."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortThreeValueArray(std::vector<int>& data) {\n\tint n = data.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < n - 1; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (data[j] > data[j+1]) {\n\t\t\t\tint temp = data[j];\n\t\t\t\tdata[j] = data[j+1];\n\t\t\t\tdata[j+1] = temp;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortThreeValueArray(std::vector<int>& data) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = (int)data.size() - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (data[mid] == 0) {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(data[low], data[mid]);\n\t\t\tlow++;\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else if (data[mid] == 1) {\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(data[mid], data[high]);\n\t\t\thigh--;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nBubble sort's O(n^2) complexity is replaced by the O(n) Dutch National Flag algorithm. This optimization provides a significant speedup for datasets containing only three unique values (0, 1, 2) by organizing the entire vector in a single pass with minimal data movement."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortIntsSelection(std::vector<int>& vec) {\n\tint n = vec.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tint m = i;\n\t\tfor (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (vec[j] < vec[m]) m = j;\n\t\t}\n\t\tstd::swap(vec[i], vec[m]);\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortIntsSelection(std::vector<int>& vec) {\n\tstd::sort(vec.begin(), vec.end());\n}\n---\nSelection sort's O(n^2) complexity is replaced with std::sort's O(n log n). Standard library sorts are much more efficient for integer arrays, using highly tuned introsort logic that maximizes throughput and provides much better scaling for larger datasets than manual loops."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <string>\n\nstruct LogRecord {\n\tint priority;\n\tint timestamp;\n};\n\nvoid sortLogs(std::vector<LogRecord>& logs) {\n\tint n = logs.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tfor (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (logs[i].priority > logs[j].priority) {\n\t\t\t\tstd::swap(logs[i], logs[j]);\n\t\t\t} else if (logs[i].priority == logs[j].priority && logs[i].timestamp > logs[j].timestamp) {\n\t\t\t\tstd::swap(logs[i], logs[j]);\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstruct LogRecord {\n\tint priority;\n\tint timestamp;\n};\n\nvoid sortLogs(std::vector<LogRecord>& logs) {\n\tstd::sort(logs.begin(), logs.end(), [](const LogRecord& a, const LogRecord& b) {\n\t\tif (a.priority != b.priority) return a.priority < b.priority;\n\t\treturn a.timestamp < b.timestamp;\n\t});\n}\n---\nSelection sort's O(n^2) complexity is optimized to O(n log n) using std::sort with a lambda comparator. This efficiently handles multi-field sorting of log records, providing a much faster and more scalable solution for large datasets by utilizing the standard library's optimized sorting algorithms."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortThreeDigitVec(std::vector<int>& v) {\n\tint n = v.size();\n\tfor (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tint k = v[i];\n\t\tint j = i - 1;\n\t\twhile (j >= 0 && v[j] > k) {\n\t\t\tv[j + 1] = v[j];\n\t\t\tj--;\n\t\t}\n\t\tv[j + 1] = k;\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortThreeDigitVec(std::vector<int>& v) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = (int)v.size() - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (v[mid] == 0) {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(v[low], v[mid]);\n\t\t\tlow++;\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else if (v[mid] == 1) {\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(v[mid], v[high]);\n\t\t\thigh--;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nInsertion sort's O(n^2) complexity is replaced by the O(n) Dutch National Flag algorithm. This provides an optimal linear-time solution for sorting data with only three unique values (0, 1, 2) by organizing the entire vector in a single pass with minimal comparison and data movement."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid findNthElementInVec(std::vector<int>& vec, int n) {\n\tint s = vec.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < s; i++) {\n\t\tfor (int j = i + 1; j < s; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (vec[i] > vec[j]) std::swap(vec[i], vec[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid findNthElementInVec(std::vector<int>& vec, int n) {\n\tif (n >= 0 && n < (int)vec.size()) {\n\t\tstd::nth_element(vec.begin(), vec.begin() + n, vec.end());\n\t}\n}\n---\nReplacing the O(n^2) selection sort with std::nth_element reduces the average time complexity to O(n). This is much more efficient because it only partitions the array enough to place the target n-th element correctly, avoiding the unnecessary overhead of sorting the entire collection."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <string>\n\nstruct InventoryItem {\n\tint quantity;\n\tint weight;\n};\n\nvoid sortInventory(std::vector<InventoryItem>& items) {\n\tint n = items.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tfor (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (items[i].quantity > items[j].quantity) {\n\t\t\t\tstd::swap(items[i], items[j]);\n\t\t\t} else if (items[i].quantity == items[j].quantity && items[i].weight > items[j].weight) {\n\t\t\t\tstd::swap(items[i], items[j]);\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstruct InventoryItem {\n\tint quantity;\n\tint weight;\n};\n\nvoid sortInventory(std::vector<InventoryItem>& items) {\n\tstd::sort(items.begin(), items.end(), [](const InventoryItem& a, const InventoryItem& b) {\n\t\tif (a.quantity != b.quantity) return a.quantity < b.quantity;\n\t\treturn a.weight < b.weight;\n\t});\n}\n---\nSelection sort's O(n^2) complexity is replaced by std::sort's O(n log n) with a lambda. This optimization is significantly more efficient for sorting structs by multiple fields, providing much better performance on large datasets by using the standard library's robust and optimized sorting algorithm."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortThreeValueVec(std::vector<int>& vec) {\n\tint n = vec.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < n - 1; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (vec[j] > vec[j+1]) {\n\t\t\t\tint temp = vec[j];\n\t\t\t\tvec[j] = vec[j+1];\n\t\t\t\tvec[j+1] = temp;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortThreeValueVec(std::vector<int>& vec) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = (int)vec.size() - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (vec[mid] == 0) {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(vec[low], vec[mid]);\n\t\t\tlow++;\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else if (vec[mid] == 1) {\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(vec[mid], vec[high]);\n\t\t\thigh--;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nBubble sort's O(n^2) complexity is replaced by the O(n) Dutch National Flag algorithm. This provides a significant speedup for datasets with only three unique values (0, 1, 2) by organizing the data in a single pass with minimal data movement and comparisons."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortDoublesBubble(std::vector<double>& arr) {\n\tint n = arr.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < n - 1; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (arr[j] > arr[j+1]) std::swap(arr[j], arr[j+1]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortDoublesBubble(std::vector<double>& arr) {\n\tstd::sort(arr.begin(), arr.end());\n}\n---\nReplacing the O(n^2) bubble sort with std::sort (O(n log n)) improves performance significantly. Standard library sorts are highly optimized for primitive types like double, using efficient partitioning and cache-friendly access patterns that are much faster than manual loop-based approaches for large vectors."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <string>\n\nstruct ScoreItem {\n\tint score;\n\tint id;\n};\n\nvoid sortScoreItems(std::vector<ScoreItem>& items) {\n\tint n = items.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tint best = i;\n\t\tfor (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (items[j].score > items[best].score) best = j;\n\t\t}\n\t\tstd::swap(items[i], items[best]);\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstruct ScoreItem {\n\tint score;\n\tint id;\n};\n\nvoid sortScoreItems(std::vector<ScoreItem>& items) {\n\tstd::sort(items.begin(), items.end(), [](const ScoreItem& a, const ScoreItem& b) {\n\t\treturn a.score > b.score;\n\t});\n}\n---\nSelection sort's O(n^2) complexity is replaced by std::sort's O(n log n). Using a lambda comparator to sort items by score is much more efficient and scalable, leveraging the robust sorting logic of the C++ standard library for superior performance on large score lists."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortThreeValueData(std::vector<int>& vec) {\n\tint n = vec.size();\n\tfor (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tint key = vec[i];\n\t\tint j = i - 1;\n\t\twhile (j >= 0 && vec[j] > key) {\n\t\t\tvec[j + 1] = vec[j];\n\t\t\tj--;\n\t\t}\n\t\tvec[j + 1] = key;\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortThreeValueData(std::vector<int>& vec) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = (int)vec.size() - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (vec[mid] == 0) {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(vec[low], vec[mid]);\n\t\t\tlow++;\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else if (vec[mid] == 1) {\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(vec[mid], vec[high]);\n\t\t\thigh--;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nInsertion sort's O(n^2) complexity is replaced by the O(n) Dutch National Flag algorithm. This provides an optimal linear-time solution for sorting data with only three unique values (0, 1, 2) by organizing the entire vector in a single pass with minimal comparison and data movement."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid findNthSmallestInVec(std::vector<int>& arr, int n) {\n\tint size = arr.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {\n\t\tfor (int j = i + 1; j < size; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (arr[i] > arr[j]) std::swap(arr[i], arr[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid findNthSmallestInVec(std::vector<int>& arr, int n) {\n\tif (n >= 0 && n < (int)arr.size()) {\n\t\tstd::nth_element(arr.begin(), arr.begin() + n, arr.end());\n\t}\n}\n---\nReplacing the O(n^2) selection sort with std::nth_element reduces the average complexity to O(n). This is much more efficient because it only partitions the array enough to place the target n-th smallest element correctly, avoiding the unnecessary overhead of sorting the entire collection."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <string>\n\nstruct DataRecord {\n\tint key;\n\tint value;\n};\n\nvoid sortDataRecords(std::vector<DataRecord>& list) {\n\tint n = list.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tfor (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (list[i].key > list[j].key) {\n\t\t\t\tstd::swap(list[i], list[j]);\n\t\t\t} else if (list[i].key == list[j].key && list[i].value < list[j].value) {\n\t\t\t\tstd::swap(list[i], list[j]);\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstruct DataRecord {\n\tint key;\n\tint value;\n};\n\nvoid sortDataRecords(std::vector<DataRecord>& list) {\n\tstd::sort(list.begin(), list.end(), [](const DataRecord& a, const DataRecord& b) {\n\t\tif (a.key != b.key) return a.key < b.key;\n\t\treturn a.value > b.value;\n\t});\n}\n---\nSelection sort's O(n^2) complexity is replaced by std::sort's O(n log n) with a custom lambda. This optimization is much more efficient for multi-field sorting of data records, providing significantly better performance on large datasets by using the standard library's robust and optimized sorting algorithm."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortThreeValueList(std::vector<int>& list) {\n\tint n = list.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < n - 1; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (list[j] > list[j+1]) {\n\t\t\t\tint t = list[j];\n\t\t\t\tlist[j] = list[j+1];\n\t\t\t\tlist[j+1] = t;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortThreeValueList(std::vector<int>& list) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = (int)list.size() - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (list[mid] == 0) {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(list[low], list[mid]);\n\t\t\tlow++;\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else if (list[mid] == 1) {\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(list[mid], list[high]);\n\t\t\thigh--;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nBubble sort's O(n^2) complexity is replaced by the O(n) Dutch National Flag algorithm. This provides a massive speedup for datasets with only three unique values (0, 1, 2) by organizing the data in a single pass with minimal comparison and data movement."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortLongLongsBubble(std::vector<long long>& data) {\n\tint n = data.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < n - 1; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (data[j] > data[j+1]) std::swap(data[j], data[j+1]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortLongLongsBubble(std::vector<long long>& data) {\n\tstd::sort(data.begin(), data.end());\n}\n---\nReplacing the O(n^2) bubble sort with std::sort (O(n log n)) improves the performance significantly. For primitive types like long long, the standard library sort is extremely efficient, utilizing cache-friendly partitioning and low-level optimizations that are not present in naive loop-based sorts."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <string>\n\nstruct RankingRecord {\n\tint rank;\n\tint id;\n};\n\nvoid sortRankingRecords(std::vector<RankingRecord>& list) {\n\tint n = list.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tint best = i;\n\t\tfor (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (list[j].rank < list[best].rank) best = j;\n\t\t}\n\t\tstd::swap(list[i], list[best]);\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstruct RankingRecord {\n\tint rank;\n\tint id;\n};\n\nvoid sortRankingRecords(std::vector<RankingRecord>& list) {\n\tstd::sort(list.begin(), list.end(), [](const RankingRecord& a, const RankingRecord& b) {\n\t\treturn a.rank < b.rank;\n\t});\n}\n---\nSelection sort's O(n^2) complexity is replaced by std::sort's O(n log n). Using a lambda comparator to sort ranking records by rank is much more efficient and scalable, leveraging the robust sorting logic of the C++ standard library for superior performance on large lists."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortThreeValueContainer(std::vector<int>& vec) {\n\tint n = vec.size();\n\tfor (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tint key = vec[i];\n\t\tint j = i - 1;\n\t\twhile (j >= 0 && vec[j] > key) {\n\t\t\tvec[j + 1] = vec[j];\n\t\t\tj--;\n\t\t}\n\t\tvec[j + 1] = key;\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortThreeValueContainer(std::vector<int>& vec) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = (int)vec.size() - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (vec[mid] == 0) {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(vec[low], vec[mid]);\n\t\t\tlow++;\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else if (vec[mid] == 1) {\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(vec[mid], vec[high]);\n\t\t\thigh--;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nInsertion sort's O(n^2) complexity is replaced by the O(n) Dutch National Flag algorithm. This provides an optimal linear-time solution for sorting data with only three unique values (0, 1, 2) by organizing the entire vector in a single pass with minimal comparison and data movement."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid findNthElementInArray(std::vector<int>& arr, int n) {\n\tint size = arr.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {\n\t\tfor (int j = i + 1; j < size; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (arr[i] > arr[j]) std::swap(arr[i], arr[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid findNthElementInArray(std::vector<int>& arr, int n) {\n\tif (n >= 0 && n < (int)arr.size()) {\n\t\tstd::nth_element(arr.begin(), arr.begin() + n, arr.end());\n\t}\n}\n---\nReplacing the O(n^2) selection sort with std::nth_element reduces the average complexity to O(n). This is much more efficient because it only partitions the array enough to place the target n-th element correctly, avoiding the unnecessary overhead of sorting the entire collection."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <string>\n\nstruct ItemRecord {\n\tint prio;\n\tint val;\n};\n\nvoid sortItemRecords(std::vector<ItemRecord>& list) {\n\tint n = list.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tfor (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (list[i].prio > list[j].prio) {\n\t\t\t\tstd::swap(list[i], list[j]);\n\t\t\t} else if (list[i].prio == list[j].prio && list[i].val < list[j].val) {\n\t\t\t\tstd::swap(list[i], list[j]);\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstruct ItemRecord {\n\tint prio;\n\tint val;\n};\n\nvoid sortItemRecords(std::vector<ItemRecord>& list) {\n\tstd::sort(list.begin(), list.end(), [](const ItemRecord& a, const ItemRecord& b) {\n\t\tif (a.prio != b.prio) return a.prio < b.prio;\n\t\treturn a.val > b.val;\n\t});\n}\n---\nSelection sort's O(n^2) complexity is replaced by std::sort's O(n log n) with a custom lambda. This optimization is much more efficient for multi-field sorting of item records, providing significantly better performance on large datasets by using the standard library's robust and optimized sorting algorithm."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortThreeValueVector(std::vector<int>& vec) {\n\tint n = vec.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < n - 1; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (vec[j] > vec[j+1]) {\n\t\t\t\tint t = vec[j];\n\t\t\t\tvec[j] = vec[j+1];\n\t\t\t\tvec[j+1] = t;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortThreeValueVector(std::vector<int>& vec) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = (int)vec.size() - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (vec[mid] == 0) {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(vec[low], vec[mid]);\n\t\t\tlow++;\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else if (vec[mid] == 1) {\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(vec[mid], vec[high]);\n\t\t\thigh--;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nBubble sort's O(n^2) complexity is replaced by the O(n) Dutch National Flag algorithm. This provides a massive speedup for datasets with only three unique values (0, 1, 2) by organizing the data in a single pass with minimal comparison and data movement."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortFloatsSelection(std::vector<float>& arr) {\n\tint n = arr.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tint m = i;\n\t\tfor (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (arr[j] < arr[m]) m = j;\n\t\t}\n\t\tstd::swap(arr[i], arr[m]);\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortFloatsSelection(std::vector<float>& arr) {\n\tstd::sort(arr.begin(), arr.end());\n}\n---\nSelection sort's O(n^2) complexity is replaced with std::sort's O(n log n). Standard library sorts are much more efficient for floating-point arrays, using highly tuned introsort logic that maximizes throughput and provides much better scaling for larger datasets than manual loops."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <string>\n\nstruct RankItem {\n\tint rank;\n\tint value;\n};\n\nvoid sortRankItems(std::vector<RankItem>& list) {\n\tint n = list.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tint best = i;\n\t\tfor (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (list[j].rank > list[best].rank) best = j;\n\t\t}\n\t\tstd::swap(list[i], list[best]);\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstruct RankItem {\n\tint rank;\n\tint value;\n};\n\nvoid sortRankItems(std::vector<RankItem>& list) {\n\tstd::sort(list.begin(), list.end(), [](const RankItem& a, const RankItem& b) {\n\t\treturn a.rank > b.rank;\n\t});\n}\n---\nSelection sort's O(n^2) complexity is replaced by std::sort's O(n log n). Using a lambda comparator to sort rank items by rank is much more efficient and scalable, leveraging the robust sorting logic of the C++ standard library for superior performance on large lists."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n\nvoid sortThreeValueArrayVec(std::vector<int>& arr) {\n\tint n = arr.size();\n\tfor (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tint key = arr[i];\n\t\tint j = i - 1;\n\t\twhile (j >= 0 && arr[j] > key) {\n\t\t\tarr[j + 1] = arr[j];\n\t\t\tj--;\n\t\t}\n\t\tarr[j + 1] = key;\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortThreeValueArrayVec(std::vector<int>& arr) {\n\tint low = 0, mid = 0, high = (int)arr.size() - 1;\n\twhile (mid <= high) {\n\t\tif (arr[mid] == 0) {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(arr[low], arr[mid]);\n\t\t\tlow++;\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else if (arr[mid] == 1) {\n\t\t\tmid++;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tstd::swap(arr[mid], arr[high]);\n\t\t\thigh--;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n---\nInsertion sort's O(n^2) complexity is replaced by the O(n) Dutch National Flag algorithm. This provides an optimal linear-time solution for sorting data with only three unique values (0, 1, 2) by organizing the entire vector in a single pass with minimal comparison and data movement."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid findNthLargeElement(std::vector<int>& vec, int n) {\n\tint size = vec.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {\n\t\tfor (int j = i + 1; j < size; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (vec[i] < vec[j]) std::swap(vec[i], vec[j]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid findNthLargeElement(std::vector<int>& vec, int n) {\n\tif (n >= 0 && n < (int)vec.size()) {\n\t\tstd::nth_element(vec.begin(), vec.begin() + n, vec.end(), std::greater<int>());\n\t}\n}\n---\nReplacing the O(n^2) selection sort with std::nth_element reduces the average complexity to O(n). This is much more efficient because it only partitions the array enough to place the target n-th largest element correctly, avoiding the unnecessary overhead of sorting the entire collection."}
{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <string>\n\nstruct DataEntry {\n\tint key;\n\tint val;\n};\n\nvoid sortDataEntries(std::vector<DataEntry>& list) {\n\tint n = list.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tfor (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (list[i].key > list[j].key) {\n\t\t\t\tstd::swap(list[i], list[j]);\n\t\t\t} else if (list[i].key == list[j].key && list[i].val > list[j].val) {\n\t\t\t\tstd::swap(list[i], list[j]);\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstruct DataEntry {\n\tint key;\n\tint val;\n};\n\nvoid sortDataEntries(std::vector<DataEntry>& list) {\n\tstd::sort(list.begin(), list.end(), [](const DataEntry& a, const DataEntry& b) {\n\t\tif (a.key != b.key) return a.key < b.key;\n\t\treturn a.val < b.val;\n\t});\n}\n---\nSelection sort's O(n^2) complexity is replaced by std::sort's O(n log n) with a custom lambda. This optimization is much more efficient for multi-field sorting of data entries, providing significantly better performance on large datasets by using the standard library's robust and optimized sorting algorithm."}
