{"instruction": "Analyze the following C++ code for performance bottlenecks. Suggest optimized data structures and explain why.", "input": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortIntsBubbleVec(std::vector<int>& data) {\n\tint n = data.size();\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < n - 1; j++) {\n\t\t\tif (data[j] > data[j+1]) std::swap(data[j], data[j+1]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}", "output": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid sortIntsBubbleVec(std::vector<int>& data) {\n\tstd::sort(data.begin(), data.end());\n}\n---\nReplacing the O(n^2) bubble sort with std::sort (O(n log n)) improves the performance significantly. For integer arrays, the standard library sort is extremely efficient, utilizing cache-friendly partitioning and low-level optimizations that are not present in naive loop-based sorts."}
